export default [
  {
    plain: true, path: 'best-undercounter-commercial-ice-maker', short: 'Undercounter commercial ice makers', blurb: 'Commercial undercounter machines for bars, cafes and small kitchens.',
    tab: 'Best Undercounter Commercial Ice Maker (2026): Sizing and Picks',
    summary: 'Best undercounter commercial ice makers by ice type and output, with sizing rules, air vs water cooling, drain and clearance needs, and upkeep.',
    heading: 'Best undercounter commercial ice maker',
    lede: 'A commercial undercounter machine makes and stores ice in one cabinet that fits under a bar or prep counter. The right one depends on ice type, daily peak demand, and how the kitchen handles heat and water.',
    quick: 'For most bars and cafes, a Hoshizaki IM-200BAK (up to 186 lb a day, 75 lb bin) is the safe all-round cuber. Choose the Manitowoc USP0100A for clear cocktail cubes, the Manitowoc UNP0200A for nugget, and the Scotsman UC2024SWX water-cooled unit for hot kitchens. Size for your busiest day, not your average one.',
    list: ['Hoshizaki IM-200BAK', 'Manitowoc USP0100A-161 CrystalCraft', 'Manitowoc UNP0200A-161 nugget', 'Scotsman UC2024SWX-1A water-cooled', 'Avantco Ice Diamond EUC-G-80-A', 'Hoshizaki AM-50BAK'],
    parts: [
      ['Quick picks', compare('Commercial undercounter ice machines at a glance', ['hoshizakiim200', 'manitowocusp', 'manitowocunp', 'scotsmanuc2024', 'avantcoeuc', 'hoshizakiam50bak'], ['Ice', 'Daily output', 'Bin'])],
      ['Best all-round cuber', pick('hoshizakiim200', 'Best overall', 'The IM-200BAK is rated for up to 186 pounds of full-size cubes a day and holds 75 pounds in its bin, enough for steady beverage service in a busy bar, restaurant or cafe. Front air intake and discharge reduce the clearance it needs, and the removable air filter makes routine care quick.')],
      ['Best for clear cocktail ice', pick('manitowocusp', 'Best clear cubes', 'The CrystalCraft makes crystal-clear square cubes and is rated up to 100 pounds a day. It suits bars, lounges and cafes where ice quality matters as much as volume. The 38 lb bin is the limit: during a long rush you may need to wait for it to refill.')],
      ['Best nugget machine', pick('manitowocunp', 'Best commercial nugget', 'Nugget is the soft, chewable ice that self-serve drink stations and convenience stores favor. This unit is rated up to 213 pounds a day (164 at 90 F air and 70 F water) with a 30 lb bin and fits under a standard counter. Plan for more frequent cleaning than a cube machine needs.')],
      ['Best for hot kitchens', pick('scotsmanuc2024', 'Best water-cooled', 'A water-cooled condenser keeps output steadier where the room is hot or the air is greasy, because it does not depend on room airflow. The cost is water: these units use much more of it than air-cooled models, and they need a condenser water connection as well as a drain.')],
      ['Best lower-cost gourmet ice', pick('avantcoeuc', 'Best budget gourmet', 'This model makes gourmet cubes, rated up to 77 pounds a day with a 33 lb bin, and its cleaning mode circulates water and descaler without running the cooling system. It fits cafes, breakrooms and start-ups with light to moderate drink service, not a busy bar.')],
      ['Best for tight spaces', pick('hoshizakiam50bak', 'Best compact', 'At about 15 inches wide and under 34 inches tall, with front in, front out airflow, the AM-50BAK slots into narrow openings without side or rear clearance. Hoshizaki rates it up to 54 pounds a day, so it fits hotel suites, offices and low-volume kitchens.')],
      ['How much ice you need', `<p>Undercounter makers are rated for a 24 hour day, but you size them for your peak. Manufacturers commonly recommend allowing about 1.5 to 2 pounds of ice per person, then planning around the number of people served at the busiest time. The most common mistake is buying too small: operators almost never complain that a machine makes too much ice.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Typical daily output range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cafe or small office</td><td>50 to 150 lb</td></tr><tr><td>Restaurant</td><td>100 to 300 lb</td></tr><tr><td>Bar</td><td>200 to 400 lb</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These ranges come from foodservice supplier guides and are starting points. Add a buffer of 20 to 30 percent for events and seasonal peaks. Remember that rated output assumes the cool room and cool water used in the rating test; a hot kitchen cuts real output.</p>`],
      ['Choose the ice type first', `<table><thead><tr><th>Ice type</th><th>Best for</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Regular cube</td><td>Soda, tea, mixed drinks</td><td>Most versatile, slow melting</td></tr><tr><td>Gourmet or square cube</td><td>Cocktails, spirits, premium drinks</td><td>Clear, slow melting, lower output per machine</td></tr><tr><td>Nugget</td><td>Self-serve, blended drinks, healthcare</td><td>Soft and chewable, needs more cleaning</td></tr><tr><td>Flake</td><td>Food displays, salad bars</td><td>Cools fast, molds to shape</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Some operators run two machines: a gourmet unit at the front bar for specialty drinks and a larger cuber in the back for soda and tea. That also saves bartenders trips to the back of the house.</p>`],
      ['Air-cooled or water-cooled', `<p>Air-cooled units are the standard choice. They use fans to pull room air across the condenser, need no condenser water, and cost less to run, but they need clear air intake and exhaust and struggle in very hot or dusty rooms. Most newer undercounter models move the air intake and exhaust to the front so they can sit flush between cabinets; check the clearance in the spec sheet anyway, because a few older designs still need around 6 inches of air space.</p><p>Water-cooled units move heat into water, so they hold up better in hot kitchens or where grease in the air clogs condensers. They use much more water and need a water supply, a drain and municipal rules that allow them.</p>`],
      ['Drain, power and fit', `<ul><li><b>Drain.</b> Gravity drains need a drain line close by and sloping downhill. If the floor drain is not close or low enough, add a drain pump. On Hoshizaki units the pump is a separate accessory.</li><li><b>Electrical.</b> Most undercounter units run on 115 volts with a standard plug, but check each spec sheet. Larger or water-cooled units may need their own circuit.</li><li><b>Height.</b> Standard counters are 34 to 36 inches high. Adjustable legs help level the unit, and ADA counters at 32 inches need a unit built for them or a floor-mount kit.</li><li><b>Weight.</b> A full unit can weigh several hundred pounds, so check the floor and the path in.</li><li><b>Outdoors.</b> Only machines rated for outdoor use belong on a patio. Hoshizaki lists the AM series as UL approved for outdoor use, with an ambient limit of 45 to 100 F.</li></ul>`],
      ['Water filtration', `<p>Water quality decides how your ice looks and tastes and how fast scale builds up inside the machine. A carbon filter removes chlorine taste and odor; a scale inhibitor slows mineral buildup. Hard water is the main enemy of undercounter machines, and it shows up as cloudy ice, low output and early service calls. Budget for filter cartridges and replace them on the schedule printed in the manual.</p>`],
      ['Energy and certifications', `<ul><li><b>NSF.</b> Look for NSF listing on any machine going into a commercial kitchen. Local health codes usually require it.</li><li><b>ENERGY STAR.</b> The commercial ice maker program covers air-cooled batch and continuous (nugget and flake) machines, including self-contained units, with ice harvest rates from 50 to 4,000 pounds a day. ENERGY STAR says certified batch-type machines are about 10 percent more energy efficient and 20 percent more water efficient than standard ones, and certified continuous-type machines are about 16 percent more energy efficient. Water-cooled machines and dispensing systems are not eligible.</li><li><b>Refrigerant.</b> Lower-impact refrigerants such as R290 are becoming common. The Hoshizaki AM-50BAK and Manitowoc CrystalCraft both list R290.</li></ul>`],
      ['Upkeep schedule', `<table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>How often</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wipe the exterior and sanitize the bin</td><td>Daily</td></tr><tr><td>Check and replace water filters</td><td>Weekly check, replace per manual</td></tr><tr><td>Vacuum the air-cooled condenser or air filter</td><td>Monthly</td></tr><tr><td>Deep clean with commercial ice machine cleaner</td><td>Quarterly or per manual</td></tr><tr><td>Professional inspection</td><td>Yearly</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Follow the manual where it differs. For step-by-step cleaning see <a href="/how-to-clean-commercial-ice-maker/">how to clean a commercial ice maker</a>. Skipping cleaning is the quickest way to slimy ice, off flavors and a warranty problem.</p>`],
      ['Troubleshooting', `<ul><li><b>Low output.</b> Check water pressure, the filter, scale buildup, and whether the condenser or air filter is clogged. A hot room also lowers output.</li><li><b>Cloudy ice.</b> Usually hard water or an old filter. Clear cube machines depend on clean incoming water.</li><li><b>Water on the floor.</b> Check the drain line for clogs, then check that the unit is level.</li><li><b>Noisy running.</b> Loose panels, an unlevel cabinet or dirty coils.</li></ul>`],
      ['Buy, lease or rent', `<p>Commercial units are expensive enough that leasing is worth a look, especially for a new business. We compare the options in <a href="/commercial-ice-maker-rental-vs-buying/">commercial ice maker rental vs buying</a>. Whatever you choose, use a licensed installer for the first install so the warranty stays valid.</p>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['How big should an undercounter commercial ice maker be?', 'Size it for peak demand. A common rule is 1.5 to 2 pounds of ice per person served, plus a 20 to 30 percent buffer for events. If you are between sizes, buy the larger one.'],
      ['Do undercounter ice makers need a drain?', 'Yes. Commercial machines melt ice in the bin and flush water, so they need a drain. Use a gravity drain if the line can slope downhill, or add a drain pump.'],
      ['What is the difference between an undercounter ice maker and a regular countertop ice maker?', 'Undercounter commercial units are built for daily commercial use, hold their ice in an insulated bin, and are rated far above countertop machines. Countertop machines do not keep ice frozen and are not rated for commercial duty.'],
      ['Can I put a commercial undercounter ice maker in a home?', 'Yes, many home bars use them. You still need power, a water line and a drain, and a unit rated for the ambient temperature of the room.'],
      ['How long do undercounter ice machines last?', 'With regular cleaning, filter changes and annual service, commercial models are commonly expected to last 8 to 12 years.'],
      ['Is a water-cooled undercounter ice maker worth it?', 'Only if the room is hot, poorly ventilated or full of grease in the air. Otherwise an air-cooled unit costs less to run because it uses far less water, and it is simpler to install.'],
    ],
    refs: [['Hoshizaki America, AM-50BAK specifications', 'https://www.hoshizakiamerica.com/product/am-50bak/'], ['Hoshizaki America, AM-50BAJ specifications (replaced by AM-50BAK)', 'https://www.hoshizakiamerica.com/product/am-50baj/'], ['ENERGY STAR, Commercial Ice Makers', 'https://www.energystar.gov/products/commercial_ice_makers'], ['Manitowoc Ice, undercounter ice machines', 'https://www.manitowocice.com/Products/Undercounter-Ice-Machines'], ['WebstaurantStore, commercial undercounter ice machine specifications', 'https://www.webstaurantstore.com/guide/1384/best-commercial-undercounter-ice-machines-reviews.html'], ['GoFoodservice, undercounter ice machine buying guide', 'https://www.gofoodservice.com/guides/undercounter-ice-machine-buying-guide']],
    next: ['best-commercial-ice-maker', 'best-undercounter-ice-maker', 'manitowoc-ice-machine-review', 'hoshizaki-ice-machine-review', 'how-to-clean-commercial-ice-maker', 'commercial-ice-maker-rental-vs-buying'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'best-ice-maker-for-coffee-shop', short: 'Ice makers for coffee shops', blurb: 'Sizing and picks for cafes, from undercounter cubers to nugget dispensers.',
    tab: 'Best Ice Maker for a Coffee Shop (2026): Size, Ice Type, Picks',
    summary: 'Best ice maker for a coffee shop: how to size for iced drinks, which ice type suits coffee, blended drinks and self-serve, and the commercial machines worth buying.',
    heading: 'Best ice maker for a coffee shop',
    lede: 'Iced drinks can make up most of a cafe menu, so the ice machine is as important as the espresso machine. Pick the ice type for your drinks, then size the machine for your busiest hour.',
    quick: 'Most independent coffee shops are best served by an undercounter commercial machine: the Hoshizaki IM-200BAK for standard cubes, the Manitowoc CrystalCraft for clear premium cubes, or a nugget machine such as the Manitowoc UNP0200A if you sell blended drinks and self-serve cups. Size it with this formula: daily drinks x cold share x pounds of ice per drink x 1.2.',
    list: ['Hoshizaki IM-200BAK', 'Manitowoc USP0100A-161 CrystalCraft', 'Manitowoc UNP0200A-161 nugget', 'Hoshizaki DCM-271BAK cubelet dispenser', 'Avantco Ice Diamond EUC-G-80-A'],
    parts: [
      ['Quick picks', compare('Ice machines for coffee shops', ['hoshizakiim200', 'manitowocusp', 'manitowocunp', 'hoshizakidcm271', 'avantcoeuc'], ['Ice', 'Daily output', 'Bin'])],
      ['Size it for your menu', `<p>Rated output is for 24 hours, but a cafe uses most of its ice in a few rush hours. Work through four numbers:</p><ol><li><b>Daily drinks.</b> Use your busiest normal day, not an average.</li><li><b>Cold share.</b> The fraction of drinks served over ice or blended. Use your own sales mix.</li><li><b>Pounds of ice per cold drink.</b> Measure it: fill a cup the way your baristas do and weigh the ice. Blended drinks use more, a small iced Americano less.</li><li><b>Safety factor.</b> Multiply by 1.2 for summer, weekends and growth.</li></ol><table><thead><tr><th>Example (illustrative inputs)</th><th>Math</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200 drinks a day, 60 percent cold, 0.5 lb per drink</td><td>200 x 0.6 x 0.5 x 1.2</td><td>72 lb a day</td></tr><tr><td>400 drinks a day, 70 percent cold, 0.6 lb per drink</td><td>400 x 0.7 x 0.6 x 1.2</td><td>about 202 lb a day</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These inputs are examples, not benchmarks; replace them with your own. Then compare the result to a machine's rated output with room to spare, because ratings assume standard test conditions. Hoshizaki, for instance, quotes its energy and water use at 90 F air and 70 F water, and a hot bar area can cut real output. Equipment makers say the most common mistake is buying too small.</p><p>Also check bin size. A 10 lb dispenser bin is fine if the machine refills fast, but a 38 lb or 75 lb bin gives you a cushion for the lunch rush.</p>`],
      ['Pick the ice for your drinks', `<table><thead><tr><th>Drink</th><th>Good ice choice</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Iced coffee, iced latte, cold brew</td><td>Regular or gourmet cubes</td><td>Cubes melt slowly and hold their shape under hot espresso</td></tr><tr><td>Blended drinks, frappes, smoothies</td><td>Nugget or cubelet</td><td>Soft ice blends smoothly and is easy on blender blades</td></tr><tr><td>Self-serve cold drinks</td><td>Nugget or cubelet</td><td>Customers like chewable ice, and it works well in dispensers</td></tr><tr><td>Premium cocktails, mocktails</td><td>Clear gourmet cubes</td><td>Large clear cubes melt slowly and look good</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ice type is also a brand choice. Some shops draw customers just for the chewy ice, and some run a gourmet unit up front for specialty drinks and a larger cuber in back for everything else.</p>`],
      ['Best all-round for a cafe', pick('hoshizakiim200', 'Best overall', 'Up to 186 pounds a day and a 75 lb bin is enough for a busy cafe that mostly serves iced coffee and tea. Front air intake and discharge let it sit flush in a cabinet, and the removable air filter takes seconds to clean.')],
      ['Best for premium iced drinks', pick('manitowocusp', 'Best clear ice', 'Clear square cubes look great in cold brew and cocktails and melt slowly. Rated up to 100 pounds a day with a 38 lb bin, it suits a cafe with a premium drink list, not a high-volume drive-through.')],
      ['Best for blended drinks and self-serve', pick('manitowocunp', 'Best nugget', 'Rated up to 213 pounds a day at 70 F air and 50 F water, this undercounter machine gives you soft, chewable ice at a commercial scale. Nugget machines need more routine cleaning than cube machines, so build that into closing duties.')],
      ['Best counter dispenser', pick('hoshizakidcm271', 'Best dispenser', 'A push-button dispenser keeps ice enclosed until it is dispensed, which reduces handling and cross-contamination, and it dispenses water too. At up to 286 pounds a day it can supply a self-serve station. The 10 lb bin means you should check refill speed against your rush.')],
      ['Best budget gourmet unit', pick('avantcoeuc', 'Best lower-cost option', 'For a start-up cafe that wants gourmet cubes without a big equipment bill, the Avantco is rated up to 77 pounds a day with a 33 lb bin. It will not keep pace with a heavy rush, so use it where drink volume is light to moderate.')],
      ['Undercounter, dispenser or modular', `<ul><li><b>Undercounter.</b> The usual choice for independent shops. Ice is stored under the bar within reach of the barista. Output typically ranges from about 50 to over 400 pounds a day.</li><li><b>Countertop dispenser.</b> Good when customers serve themselves, or when you want hands-free dispensing. Needs a water line and drain.</li><li><b>Modular head over a bin.</b> For very high volume or when ice also supplies a kitchen. It needs more space and installation work.</li></ul>`],
      ['Install checklist', `<ul><li>Measure the counter opening, including height. Standard counters are 34 to 36 inches high, and ADA counters can be 32 inches.</li><li>Plan the drain. A gravity drain needs a line close by that slopes downhill; otherwise add a drain pump.</li><li>Check ventilation. Heat from espresso machines, ovens and dishwashers lowers ice output.</li><li>Install a water filter. Hard water scale, cloudy ice and early service calls go together.</li><li>Use a licensed installer for the first install so the warranty stays valid.</li></ul>`],
      ['Handling ice safely', `<p>Ice served to customers is food. Safe-handling guidance from commercial insurers and health departments boils down to a few rules:</p><ul><li>Wash hands before and after handling ice.</li><li>Use a plastic ice scoop. Plastic scoops are the NSF-approved choice for ice.</li><li>Store the scoop outside the ice bin, in a clean scoop caddie.</li><li>Never scoop with a cup or glass, and never handle ice by hand.</li><li>Do not store bottles or other products in food ice.</li><li>Discard ice that has started to melt, has been contaminated, or has been used to chill bottles.</li></ul><p>Rules vary by jurisdiction, so check your local health department.</p>`],
      ['Running costs and cleaning', `<p>Running cost is mostly electricity and water. Hoshizaki lists the DCM-271BAK at 5.3 kWh and 12 gallons of potable water per 100 pounds of ice at 90 F air and 70 F water, and its AM-50BAK at 9 kWh per 100 pounds. ENERGY STAR certified batch-type ice makers are about 10 percent more energy efficient and 20 percent more water efficient than standard models, and certified nugget and flake makers are about 16 percent more energy efficient. Ask any dealer whether a model is certified.</p><p>Plan for filter changes and regular sanitizing. A typical schedule is daily bin sanitizing, weekly filter checks, monthly condenser cleaning and quarterly deep cleaning with commercial ice machine cleaner, plus yearly professional service. See <a href="/how-to-clean-commercial-ice-maker/">how to clean a commercial ice maker</a>.</p>`],
      ['Rent, lease or buy', `<p>A new cafe can often lease an ice machine with service included, which turns a large purchase into a monthly cost. We compare the trade-offs in <a href="/commercial-ice-maker-rental-vs-buying/">commercial ice maker rental vs buying</a>. For more machines of this class, see <a href="/best-undercounter-commercial-ice-maker/">the best undercounter commercial ice makers</a>.</p>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['What size ice machine does a coffee shop need?', 'Multiply daily drinks by your cold share and the pounds of ice per drink, then add 20 percent. A neighborhood cafe may land near 50 to 100 pounds a day, while a busy shop selling hundreds of cold drinks needs more. Measure your own ice per drink.'],
      ['What is the best ice for iced coffee?', 'Regular or gourmet cubes are the usual choice because they melt slowly. Nugget ice melts faster in hot drinks but is popular for blended and self-serve drinks.'],
      ['Can a coffee shop use a countertop home ice maker?', 'It is not a good idea. Home countertop makers are not rated for commercial duty and do not keep ice frozen, and health inspectors expect commercial equipment with NSF listing.'],
      ['Do I need a drain for a cafe ice machine?', 'Yes. Commercial machines need a drain. If a gravity drain will not work, add a drain pump.'],
      ['Should a coffee shop get a nugget ice machine?', 'If you sell blended drinks or let customers fill their own cups, yes. If most drinks are iced espresso over cubes, a cuber or gourmet machine usually serves you better.'],
      ['How often should a coffee shop clean its ice machine?', 'Sanitize the bin and wipe the exterior daily, check filters weekly, clean the condenser monthly and run a full deep clean quarterly, or follow the interval in your manual if it is stricter. Nugget machines need more frequent cleaning.'],
      ['Is it better to lease or buy an ice machine for a new cafe?', 'Leasing cuts the upfront cost and often bundles service, which suits a new shop with tight cash. Buying costs less over many years if you maintain the machine well.'],
    ],
    refs: [['Hoshizaki America, DCM-271BAK specifications', 'https://www.hoshizakiamerica.com/product/dcm-271bak/'], ['Hoshizaki America, AM-50BAK specifications', 'https://www.hoshizakiamerica.com/product/am-50bak/'], ['ENERGY STAR, Commercial Ice Makers', 'https://www.energystar.gov/products/commercial_ice_makers'], ['WebstaurantStore, undercounter ice machine specifications', 'https://www.webstaurantstore.com/guide/1384/best-commercial-undercounter-ice-machines-reviews.html'], ['Markel, safe ice handling', 'https://www.markel.com/insights-and-resources/insights/safe-ice-handling'], ['GoFoodservice, undercounter ice machine guide', 'https://www.gofoodservice.com/guides/undercounter-ice-machine-buying-guide']],
    next: ['best-undercounter-commercial-ice-maker', 'best-commercial-ice-maker', 'best-commercial-nugget-ice-maker', 'best-commercial-ice-maker-with-dispenser', 'how-to-clean-commercial-ice-maker', 'commercial-ice-maker-rental-vs-buying'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'best-french-door-refrigerator-with-ice-maker', short: 'French door fridges with ice makers', blurb: 'How to choose a French door refrigerator by width, ice features and water line.',
    tab: 'Best French Door Refrigerator With Ice Maker: How to Choose',
    summary: 'How to pick the best French door refrigerator with an ice maker: width classes, ice features, water line needs, and when a countertop nugget maker wins.',
    heading: 'Best French door refrigerator with ice maker',
    lede: 'French door refrigerators are the most popular layout for a reason, and most come with an ice maker. This guide covers what to compare so you buy the right one for your kitchen and your ice habits.',
    quick: 'Pick the width your opening allows first: 36-inch models offer the most space and efficiency, 33-inch models are a good compromise, and 30-inch models fit tight kitchens. Then choose the ice features you will use: a standard door dispenser, a second ice maker, or craft (sphere) ice. If you want nugget ice, add a countertop machine like the GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra, because true nugget ice from a fridge door is rare.',
    list: ['36-inch French door refrigerators', '33-inch French door refrigerators', '30-inch French door refrigerators', 'GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra (nugget add-on)'],
    parts: [
      ['Choose by width and capacity', `<p>French door models account for more than half of the new refrigerators bought by members of one large consumer testing group, and they are generally bigger and more feature-packed than top-freezer or bottom-freezer models. Width and capacity are the first filters.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Width</th><th>Typical capacity</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>36 in</td><td>Over 30 cu ft (some standard-depth)</td><td>Large households, open kitchens. Bigger units tend to be more energy efficient.</td></tr><tr><td>33 in</td><td>Up to about 25 cu ft</td><td>Kitchens that cannot take 36 in. Few models exist, and they are more efficient than 30 in units.</td></tr><tr><td>30 in and narrower</td><td>Up to about 22 cu ft</td><td>Small kitchens and apartments</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Consumer testing criteria worth borrowing: thermostat performance, temperature uniformity, noise, ease of use, energy efficiency, crisper performance and ice maker performance. Samsung, for example, lists its 4-door French door line at 23 to 29 cubic feet and 30 to 33 inches wide, so the same width class holds a wide range of capacity.</p><p>Standard-depth models stick out past the counter; counter-depth models sit flush but hold less. GE lists a 36-inch Profile French door model at 69 7/8 inches high, 35 3/4 inches wide and 36 1/4 inches deep, so measure doors, hallways and the swing space as well as the opening.</p>`],
      ['Ice features to compare', `<table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it does</th><th>Worth it when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Door ice and water dispenser</td><td>Cubed or crushed ice and filtered water from the door</td><td>You want chilled water and ice without opening the door</td></tr><tr><td>Internal ice maker only</td><td>Ice bin inside the freezer, no dispenser</td><td>You want more usable door space and fewer parts</td></tr><tr><td>Dual ice makers</td><td>A second maker in the fresh food door or compartment</td><td>You entertain, or use a lot of ice. Samsung and LG both offer this.</td></tr><tr><td>Craft or sphere ice</td><td>Slow-frozen, round, clear ice</td><td>You drink cocktails or spirits. LG lists four types of ice on some models.</td></tr><tr><td>Hands-free autofill</td><td>Fills a pitcher or glass automatically</td><td>You fill pitchers often</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Fridge ice makers produce a lot less than dedicated machines. One GE French door manual says its maker produces about 100 to 130 cubes in 24 hours, depending on freezer temperature, room temperature and how often the doors are opened. That is fine for a family, but a party can run through a bin fast. Pair a fridge with an undercounter or countertop ice maker if you entertain; see <a href="/best-refrigerator-with-ice-maker/">choosing a refrigerator with an ice maker</a> for the full comparison.</p>`],
      ['If you want nugget ice', `<p>Standard refrigerator ice makers make cubes or crushed ice. Nugget ice, the soft chewable kind, comes from a dedicated machine. Our top pick for it is the Opal 2.0 Ultra, which sits on a counter beside the fridge and refills its bin on its own.</p>${pick('opalultra', 'Best nugget add-on for a fridge kitchen', 'GE lists up to 38 pounds of nugget ice a day, a 3-pound bin, and a side tank that cuts refills. It delivers what almost no French door fridge does: soft, chewable ice on demand.')}`],
      ['Water line and installation', `<ul><li><b>Water supply.</b> A cold potable water line is needed for the ice maker. One GE manual specifies water pressure between 40 and 120 psi for automatic ice maker operation; check the manual for the model you buy. Never connect it to hot water.</li><li><b>Water hammer.</b> Banging pipes can damage fridge parts. Have a plumber fix it before installing the line.</li><li><b>First ice.</b> A new refrigerator may take 12 to 24 hours to start making ice, and the bin can take a day or two to fill. GE advises throwing away the first 24 hours of ice production to clear the water line and avoid odor or taste problems.</li><li><b>Before the water is connected.</b> Turn the ice maker off so it does not run dry.</li><li><b>Warranty.</b> GE notes that the water line installation itself is not covered by the refrigerator warranty.</li></ul>`],
      ['Filters and ice quality', `<p>The water filter shapes how your ice tastes. Replace it on the schedule in your manual, and check the replacement filter price before you buy, since it is a recurring cost. Old ice also goes stale: GE notes that ice that is not used often becomes cloudy, tastes stale and shrinks. Empty the bin and let it refill if you have been away.</p>`],
      ['A short buying checklist', `<ol><li>Measure the opening, the door swing, and the path into the house.</li><li>Pick width and depth, then compare capacity at that size.</li><li>Choose the ice features you will use, and skip the rest.</li><li>Confirm a cold water line is within reach.</li><li>Check the filter price and the warranty on the ice maker and sealed system.</li><li>Decide whether you need nugget or party-size ice, and plan a separate machine if so.</li></ol>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['Do all French door refrigerators have ice makers?', 'Most do, but not all. Some models have only a freezer ice bin with no door dispenser, and some skip the ice maker entirely. Check the exact model number.'],
      ['How much ice does a French door refrigerator make?', 'It varies by model and conditions. One GE French door manual lists about 100 to 130 cubes in 24 hours.'],
      ['Can a French door refrigerator make nugget ice?', 'Almost never. Standard refrigerator ice makers make cubes or crushed ice, and true nugget ice from a fridge is rare. For nugget ice, a countertop nugget ice maker is the dependable route.'],
      ['Does a refrigerator ice maker need a water line?', 'Yes, for automatic operation. Plan a cold water line within reach of the refrigerator, and follow the manual for pressure and tubing.'],
    ],
    refs: [['GE Appliances, Profile French door refrigerator use and care manual (ice maker, water line)', 'https://assets.wfcdn.com/dm/document/53f866e7-87bf-4d51-a870-0e7a1ae5cd54/39492-4779311-pad28bytfs-use-and-care-manual-english.pdf'], ['GE Appliances, Profile 27.7 cu ft French door refrigerator dimensions', 'https://www.geappliances.com/appliance/GE-Profile-Series-ENERGY-STAR-27-7-Cu-Ft-French-Door-Refrigerator-with-Hands-Free-AutoFill-PFE28KMKES'], ['Consumer Reports, French door refrigerator testing and width classes', 'https://www.consumerreports.org/appliances/refrigerators/best-french-door-refrigerators-from-consumer-reports-tests-a5001373730/'], ['Samsung, 4-door Flex refrigerators', 'https://www.samsung.com/us/home-appliances/refrigerators/4-door-flex/'], ['GE Appliances, Opal nugget ice makers', 'https://www.geappliances.com/ge/icemakers/opal-nugget-icemaker.htm']],
    next: ['best-refrigerator-with-ice-maker', 'best-refrigerator-with-nugget-ice', 'best-nugget-ice-maker', 'ge-profile-opal-2-review', 'best-side-by-side-refrigerator-with-ice-maker'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'best-ice-maker-for-home-bar', short: 'Ice makers for a home bar', blurb: 'Clear, nugget and undercounter ice makers for cocktails and entertaining.',
    tab: 'Best Ice Maker for a Home Bar (2026): Clear, Nugget, Undercounter',
    summary: 'The best ice maker for a home bar: clear cube, nugget and undercounter machines compared, with party sizing, drain options, install tips and care.',
    heading: 'Best ice maker for a home bar',
    lede: 'Ice is a cocktail ingredient, so the best ice maker for a home bar depends on the drinks you pour and the crowd you pour for. This guide matches ice type and machine style to how you entertain.',
    quick: 'For serious cocktails, an undercounter clear ice maker such as the Euhomy 100 lb model (up to 100 lb a day, 33 lb bin) gives you the most ice and the best-looking cubes. For a small bar on a counter, the Silonn clear ice maker makes large clear cubes and the GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra makes soft nugget ice for tiki and highball drinks. Choose built-in only if you can run a drain.',
    list: ['Euhomy 100 lb Undercounter Ice Maker', 'Hoshizaki AM-50BAK', 'Scotsman CU50GA', 'KitchenAid 15 in Automatic Ice Maker', 'Silonn Clear Ice Cube Maker Countertop', 'NewAir ClearIce40', 'GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra', 'Whynter UIM-155'],
    parts: [
      ['Picks at a glance', compare('Home bar ice makers compared', ['euhomy100', 'hoshizakiam50bak', 'scotsman', 'kitchenaid15', 'newairclear', 'opalultra', 'whynter155'], ['Ice', 'Daily output', 'Bin', 'Price band'])],
      ['Which ice fits which drink', `<table><thead><tr><th>Ice</th><th>Best in</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Clear large cubes</td><td>Old fashioneds, whiskey, negronis, stirred drinks</td><td>Clear ice is built in layers so air and impurities are pushed out. It looks good and melts slowly, so the drink stays cold with less dilution.</td></tr><tr><td>Regular or gourmet cubes</td><td>Shaken drinks, long drinks, highballs</td><td>Hard cubes chill fast without breaking apart in a shaker.</td></tr><tr><td>Nugget</td><td>Tiki drinks, mint juleps, blended drinks, sodas</td><td>Soft, chewable, and it soaks up the flavor of the drink. It melts faster than a large cube.</td></tr><tr><td>Bullet</td><td>Casual drinks and big pitchers</td><td>Cheap and fast, but cloudy and melts quickly.</td></tr><tr><td>Crescent</td><td>General use, mixed drinks</td><td>Common in built-in residential machines.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A large surface area melts faster, so big cubes are the usual choice for slow sipping. Many home bars run one machine for clear sipping cubes and keep a countertop nugget maker for casual drinks.</p>`],
      ['Best overall for a home bar', pick('euhomy100', 'Best overall', 'An undercounter machine is what most serious home bars end up with, because it makes a lot of ice and stores it in one place. This 15-inch model is rated up to 100 pounds a day with a 33 lb bin, works freestanding or built in, and needs a drain or a pump version. Ice from commercial-style machines like this is clear, hard cubes rather than soft nuggets.')],
      ['Best compact undercounter', pick('hoshizakiam50bak', 'Best for tight bars', 'Hoshizaki rates it up to 54 pounds a day with a 27 lb bin, in a cabinet 14 7/8 inches wide and 33 1/2 inches tall. It is UL approved for outdoor use and needs no side or rear clearance, so it works in a built-in bar, an outdoor kitchen or a garage bar. The drain pump is an optional accessory.')],
      ['Best premium undercounter', pick('scotsman', 'Best for entertaining at scale', 'Scotsman is a commercial brand, and this model makes crystal-clear gourmet cubes with high output for a home unit. It suits large bars and frequent parties, but it needs a drain or pump and costs far more than the entry models.')],
      ['Best built-in for kitchens', pick('kitchenaid15', 'Best for a high-end kitchen', 'A premium 15-inch machine with panel-ready and stainless options, quiet running and clear cubes. Plan a professional install, a drain or pump, and a larger budget.')],
      ['Best countertop clear ice', pick('silonnclear', 'Best clear ice on a counter', 'Makes 1.6 inch clear cubes, three at a time in about 20 minutes, for roughly 26 pounds a day. Set the cube thickness to thin, medium or thick. It is a good fit for a small bar or a whiskey fan who wants a few good cubes, not a party supply.')],
      ['Best for a party-sized countertop', pick('newairclear', 'Best countertop clear cubes', 'NewAir sells a countertop machine that makes clear square cubes, with a rated output of 40 pounds a day. It costs more than bullet-ice makers but needs no drain and no built-in cabinet.')],
      ['Best nugget for the bar', pick('opalultra', 'Best nugget ice', 'GE lists up to 38 pounds a day, a 3 lb bin and a side tank that cuts refills. Nugget ice suits tiki drinks, juleps and soda. It melts faster than large cubes, so it is not the best choice for slow sipping.')],
      ['Best no-drain built-in', pick('whynter155', 'Best for small spaces', 'Whynter lists up to 12 pounds of crescent ice a day, a 6 lb bin, and no drain required, with a 15 x 18 x 25 inch cabinet. Output is low, so it suits a small bar for two, not a party.')],
      ['Countertop, undercounter or built-in', `<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Output</th><th>Needs</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Countertop</td><td>Roughly 26 to 40 lb a day</td><td>Counter space, an outlet and a few refills</td><td>Small bars, renters, apartments</td></tr><tr><td>Freestanding undercounter</td><td>About 12 to 100 lb a day</td><td>Floor space, a drain or pump, ventilation</td><td>Garages, game rooms, rental flexibility</td></tr><tr><td>Built-in undercounter</td><td>About 25 to 65 lb a day</td><td>A cabinet opening, a drain or pump, a water line</td><td>Permanent bars and kitchens</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Countertop machines do not keep ice frozen. Ice in the basket slowly melts and the water is recycled, so move it to a freezer if you will not use it soon. Undercounter machines keep ice in an insulated bin, but the bin is not a freezer either, and ice will still shrink over days.</p>`],
      ['Drain, pump or drainless', `<ul><li><b>Gravity drain.</b> The simplest. Needs a drain line that slopes down to a floor drain, a laundry standpipe or a sink drain.</li><li><b>Drain pump.</b> Pushes melt water up and out when a gravity run is not possible. Some models include it, others sell it as an accessory.</li><li><b>Drainless.</b> The machine holds melt water for you to empty, or recycles it. These are easy to place but give up some bin capacity, and the melt water tank needs emptying. The Whynter needs no drain. The COTLIN 15 in drainless model has a larger bin, but its melt water must be emptied.</li></ul><p>If you cannot run a drain at all, buy a countertop or a drainless unit rather than a drain-dependent built-in.</p>`],
      ['How much ice a party needs', `<p>Equipment makers recommend planning 1.5 to 2 pounds of ice per person for foodservice. Use that as a ceiling for a home party and cut it if the drinks are mostly stirred and served on large cubes.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Guests</th><th>Ice at 1.5 lb each</th><th>What fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10</td><td>15 lb</td><td>Any machine on this page</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>about 38 lb</td><td>A countertop machine plus a freezer bag, or an undercounter unit</td></tr><tr><td>50</td><td>75 lb</td><td>A 50 lb a day undercounter with a full bin, or a 100 lb unit</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Make ice the day before. A machine rated at 100 pounds a day fills its bin overnight, while a countertop machine making a few pounds an hour cannot keep up with a crowd. For events, bag the ice in the freezer ahead of time.</p>`],
      ['Water, filters and clarity', `<p>Clear ice needs clean water. Minerals and chlorine affect taste and cause cloudiness and scale. Use a carbon filter for taste and a scale inhibitor if your water is hard. GE includes a scale-inhibiting filter with the Opal 2.0 Ultra. Whatever you buy, replace the filter on the schedule in the manual. Distilled or filtered water helps countertop machines, and it can reduce how often you descale.</p>`],
      ['Install and fit', `<ul><li>Measure the cabinet opening width, depth and height. Standard counters run 34 to 36 inches high.</li><li>Check ventilation. A built-in needs front venting, or clearance at the sides and back if the manual says so.</li><li>Check the ambient range. Hoshizaki lists 45 to 100 F for the AM series, so a hot garage may need a different unit.</li><li>Plan a water line near the bar.</li><li>Level the unit front to back and side to side before the first cycle, and run a full cleaning cycle before you serve the first ice.</li></ul>`],
      ['Keeping it clean', `<p>Home bar ice machines get less use than restaurant units, so cleaning is easy to forget. Descale and sanitize on the schedule in your manual, and clean the bin whenever you smell a stale or musty odor. For Opals, see <a href="/how-to-clean-ge-opal-ice-maker/">how to clean a GE Opal</a>, and for countertop machines in general see <a href="/how-to-clean-portable-ice-maker/">how to clean a portable ice maker</a>. A commercial-style unit can use the steps in <a href="/how-to-clean-commercial-ice-maker/">how to clean a commercial ice maker</a>.</p>`],
      ['Common mistakes', `<ul><li>Buying a countertop machine for a party. Output per hour is low.</li><li>Buying a built-in with no drain plan.</li><li>Expecting nugget ice to behave like a large clear cube in a rocks glass.</li><li>Skipping filters, then blaming the machine for cloudy ice.</li><li>Installing in a hot space without checking the ambient range.</li><li>Storing ice for weeks. Ice absorbs freezer odors.</li></ul>`],
      ['Cocktail ice by technique', `<ul><li><b>Stirred drinks</b> (old fashioned, Manhattan, negroni): chill the glass and the liquid with large clear cubes. Big cubes melt slowly, so the last sip tastes like the first. A 2 inch cube from a countertop clear maker works; so does a standard cube from an undercounter clear machine.</li><li><b>Shaken drinks</b> (daiquiri, margarita, sour): use hard regular or gourmet cubes in the shaker. They stay intact under hard shaking, and you strain the used ice out. Nugget ice breaks down and over-dilutes in a shaker.</li><li><b>Highballs and tall drinks</b> (gin and tonic, Collins): fill the glass with cubes to slow melting.</li><li><b>Tiki and blended drinks</b>: nugget ice blends smoothly and gives the pebble-ice texture those drinks are known for.</li><li><b>Crushed ice drinks</b> (juleps, smashes): crush hard cubes in a canvas bag, or use nugget ice straight from the machine. See <a href="/best-crushed-ice-maker/">ice makers for crushed ice</a>.</li></ul><p>Chill the glass first. Warm glassware melts ice faster than any machine setting can fix.</p>`],
      ['Budget tiers', `<table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>What you get</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entry (countertop bullet)</td><td>Fast, cheap bullet ice in minutes</td><td>Cloudy, melts quickly, small batches. See <a href="/best-countertop-ice-maker/">countertop ice makers</a>.</td></tr><tr><td>Mid (countertop clear or nugget)</td><td>Large clear cubes or Opal-style nugget ice</td><td>Low output per hour, no freezer function</td></tr><tr><td>Upper (undercounter clear)</td><td>Dozens of pounds a day, insulated bin, clear cubes</td><td>Needs a drain or pump, more space</td></tr><tr><td>Premium (built-in, commercial brand)</td><td>Quiet, panel-ready or high-output commercial machines</td><td>Highest cost, professional install</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Skip tiers you will not use. Someone who hosts twice a year does not need a 100 pound machine, and someone who hosts weekly will outgrow a countertop clear maker in a month.</p>`],
      ['Running cost', `<p>Energy cost is small compared with the purchase price. Hoshizaki lists the AM-50BAK at 9 kWh per 100 pounds of ice at 90 F air and 70 F water. At an example rate of 15 cents per kWh, 30 pounds of ice a day (9 kWh per 100 lb x 0.3 = 2.7 kWh) costs about 40 cents a day, so roughly 12 dollars a month if you ran it at that rate every day. Your rate and your real usage will differ, and a hot room raises consumption.</p><p>The costs that matter more are filters, descaler or cleaner, and the water line. Budget for new filter cartridges and a cleaner designed for ice machines. For countertop machines, scale-removing products are described in <a href="/best-ice-maker-cleaning-solution/">best ice maker cleaners</a>.</p>`],
      ['Setting up the bar', `<ul><li><b>Put ice within reach.</b> Place the machine where you pour, not across the room. An undercounter unit under the bar top puts ice at hip height.</li><li><b>Use a scoop, not a glass.</b> A plastic scoop kept outside the bin is a hygiene habit worth keeping at home too.</li><li><b>Keep a dry towel and a tray under countertop machines.</b> Melt water and condensation add up.</li><li><b>Add a bucket for events.</b> A bucket on the bar keeps the machine closed and the ice cold.</li><li><b>Leave room around the unit.</b> A cramped cabinet raises temperatures and lowers output.</li><li><b>Mind the noise.</b> Compressors and pumps run intermittently, and nugget machines can be noisy. Place them where conversation is not affected.</li></ul>`],
      ['Troubleshooting a bar ice machine', `<table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>What to try</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cloudy ice from a clear ice maker</td><td>Hard water, an old filter, or a dirty machine</td><td>Replace the filter, use filtered or distilled water in countertop units, run a cleaning cycle</td></tr><tr><td>Low output</td><td>Hot room, clogged air filter, scale, low water pressure</td><td>Improve ventilation, clean the condenser or air filter, descale, check the supply line</td></tr><tr><td>Water on the floor</td><td>Clogged or kinked drain, unlevel unit</td><td>Clear the drain line, level the cabinet, check connections</td></tr><tr><td>Strange taste or smell</td><td>Dirty bin or stale ice</td><td>Sanitize, replace the filter, discard old ice</td></tr><tr><td>Unit will not start</td><td>Power, tripped breaker, bin full, door ajar</td><td>Check the outlet and the bin full sensor</td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['Who should skip a dedicated bar ice maker', `<p>If you host a few times a year, a bag of ice and a couple of silicone molds for large cubes cost far less and take no space. A dedicated machine pays off when you pour drinks every week, entertain groups, or care about ice quality enough to filter water and clean the machine regularly. If you rent, favor a countertop or drainless unit you can take with you.</p>`],
      ['Ice on hand vs ice per hour', `<p>Two numbers decide how a machine behaves at a party: how much ice is waiting in the bin when guests arrive, and how fast it makes more. The first is the bin size; the second is the daily rating divided by 24. The math below uses the ratings on this page and 1.5 pounds of ice per guest as a rough planning figure.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Machine</th><th>Bin</th><th>Guests served from a full bin</th><th>Refill per hour (rated lb a day / 24)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Euhomy 100 lb undercounter</td><td>33 lb</td><td>about 22</td><td>about 4.2 lb</td></tr><tr><td>Scotsman CU50GA</td><td>26 lb</td><td>about 17</td><td>about 2.7 lb</td></tr><tr><td>Hoshizaki AM-50BAK</td><td>27 lb</td><td>18</td><td>2.25 lb</td></tr><tr><td>KitchenAid 15 in</td><td>25 lb</td><td>about 16</td><td>about 2.1 lb</td></tr><tr><td>GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra</td><td>3 lb</td><td>2</td><td>about 1.6 lb (GE rates 1.6 lb an hour)</td></tr><tr><td>Whynter UIM-155</td><td>6 lb</td><td>4</td><td>0.5 lb</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The pattern is clear: a countertop machine cannot serve a crowd on its own, and even a good undercounter unit needs the bin full when the doors open. Start the machine a day early and keep a freezer bag or two in reserve for the peak.</p>`],
      ['Steps to install an undercounter machine', `<ol><li><b>Confirm the opening.</b> Check width, depth and height against the spec sheet, including any legs and the door swing.</li><li><b>Set the drain.</b> Run a drain line that slopes down, or install the pump. Keep the line free of kinks and low spots that trap water.</li><li><b>Connect water.</b> Fit a shutoff valve, run the supply line to the inlet and check for leaks. Install a filter on the supply line if the machine does not include one.</li><li><b>Level the cabinet.</b> Use the adjustable legs, front to back and side to side.</li><li><b>Plug in.</b> Use a grounded outlet that is not shared with other heavy appliances, and follow the manual on voltage.</li><li><b>Run a cleaning cycle</b> and discard the first batches of ice.</li><li><b>Check ice quality and output</b> over the next 24 hours before the first event.</li></ol><p>A licensed plumber or appliance installer can handle the first install and protect your warranty.</p>`],
      ['Questions to ask before you buy', `<ul><li>What type of ice does it make, and is it clear?</li><li>What is the rated daily output, and at what water and room temperature?</li><li>How big is the bin, and does it keep ice frozen or only cold?</li><li>Does it need a drain, a pump or neither, and is the pump included?</li><li>Is it rated for outdoor or garage use?</li><li>What is the cleaning cycle, and how often does the manual say to run it?</li><li>What filter does it use, and how much do replacements cost?</li><li>What does the warranty cover, and for how long?</li><li>Can you service it locally?</li></ul>`],
      ['Brand notes', `<ul><li><b>Hoshizaki, Scotsman, Manitowoc.</b> Commercial brands with strong service networks. Their undercounter units are built for heavy use and give the most predictable results, at a higher price.</li><li><b>KitchenAid and other appliance brands.</b> Quiet, styled for kitchens and sold through appliance dealers, with premium pricing.</li><li><b>Euhomy, Silonn, NewAir and Whynter.</b> Direct-to-consumer and value brands. They offer a lot of ice for the price, with thinner service networks. Check the warranty terms before you buy.</li><li><b>GE Profile.</b> The Opal is the household name for nugget ice, and GE lists the Ultra with an app, a side tank and a scale-inhibiting filter.</li></ul>`],
      ['Outdoor bars and garages', `<p>Heat and cold both hurt ice makers. Most residential and commercial units list an ambient operating range; Hoshizaki's AM series lists 45 to 100 F and water temperatures of 45 to 95 F, and the AM-50BAK is UL approved for outdoor use. Below the low end the machine may not run, and above the top end output falls and the compressor works harder. For an outdoor bar:</p><ul><li>Buy a unit specifically rated for outdoor use. Indoor machines are not designed for rain, dust or direct sun.</li><li>Shade the unit and keep its air intake clear of leaves and grease.</li><li>In cold climates, drain and shut off the water line before a freeze.</li><li>Keep the drain downhill, away from the patio surface, and protect water lines.</li><li>Clean the air filter or condenser more often than you would indoors.</li></ul><p>A countertop machine is easy to bring in for the season, which makes it the simpler choice for a patio bar. See <a href="/best-portable-ice-maker/">portable ice makers</a> for lighter options.</p>`],
      ['Pairing machines', `<p>Many bars run two machines. A single undercounter unit makes the bulk ice for shaking, long drinks and coolers. A countertop clear ice maker or Opal-style nugget machine adds the specialty ice that makes a drink feel finished. Together they cost less than one commercial gourmet unit and give you more variety. If space is limited, choose by the drinks you make most: clear cubes if you pour spirits, nugget if you pour tiki and sodas.</p>`],
      ['Why the first batches taste odd', `<p>New machines usually need a cleaning cycle and a few discarded batches before the ice tastes right. Odor and taste problems from a new water line are common enough that GE tells owners to discard the first 24 hours of refrigerator ice. Run the cycle the manual describes and discard the first batches, as GE advises for refrigerator ice makers, then check the taste before the first party.</p>`],
      ['Our recommendation', `<p>If you host often and have a place to run a drain, buy an undercounter clear cube machine. If you do not, buy a countertop clear ice maker for sipping cubes and an Opal-style nugget maker for everything else. See also <a href="/best-undercounter-ice-maker/">undercounter ice makers</a> and <a href="/best-clear-ice-maker/">clear ice makers</a>. Whatever you choose, filter the water and keep the machine clean.</p>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['What kind of ice maker is best for a home bar?', 'An undercounter machine makes the most ice and stores it, and clear cube models give the best cocktail ice. A countertop clear ice maker works for a small bar.'],
      ['Is clear ice worth it for cocktails?', 'It looks better and melts slowly, so spirit-forward drinks stay cold without becoming watery fast. For tiki drinks and soda, nugget ice is often more fun.'],
      ['Do home bar ice makers need a drain?', 'Most built-in and undercounter models do, or they come with a drain pump. A few, such as the Whynter UIM-155, need no drain but make much less ice.'],
      ['How much ice does a home bar need?', 'Plan about 1.5 to 2 pounds per guest at most. A 50 lb a day machine fills its bin overnight for a 25 to 30 person party.'],
      ['Can I use a nugget ice maker for cocktails?', 'Yes. Nugget ice chills fast and is chewable. It melts faster than large cubes, so choose it for tiki drinks, juleps and highballs.'],
      ['Can a built-in ice maker go outdoors?', 'Only if it is rated for outdoor use. Hoshizaki lists the AM series as UL approved for outdoor use within an ambient temperature range of 45 to 100 F.'],
      ['What size undercounter ice maker do I need for a home bar?', 'For a bar used weekly by a few people, a unit rated 25 to 50 pounds a day is plenty. For regular parties of 25 or more, look at 50 to 100 pounds a day and a bin of 25 pounds or more so ice is waiting when guests arrive.'],
      ['Do I need to filter water for a home bar ice maker?', 'It is strongly worth it. A carbon filter improves taste and a scale inhibitor protects the machine in hard water. Clear ice in particular depends on clean incoming water.'],
      ['Is a drainless ice maker good for a home bar?', 'It is the easiest to install, but output is usually lower and you may need to empty the melt water. Choose drainless only when you cannot run a drain.'],
    ],
    refs: [['Hoshizaki America, AM-50BAK specifications', 'https://www.hoshizakiamerica.com/product/am-50bak/'], ['Whynter, UIM-155 built-in ice maker', 'https://www.whynter.com/product/uim-155/'], ['Silonn, clear ice cube maker countertop', 'https://www.silonn.com/products/silonn-clear-ice-cube-maker-countertop'], ['GE Appliances, Opal nugget ice makers', 'https://www.geappliances.com/ge/icemakers/opal-nugget-icemaker.htm'], ['Foodservice Equipment and Supplies, choosing an undercounter ice machine', 'https://www.fermag.com/articles/pretty-clear-your-guide-to-choosing-an-undercounter-ice-machine/']],
    next: ['best-undercounter-ice-maker', 'best-clear-ice-maker', 'best-nugget-ice-maker', 'best-undercounter-commercial-ice-maker', 'best-sphere-ice-maker', 'best-freestanding-ice-maker'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'best-ice-maker-for-dorm', short: 'Ice makers for dorm rooms', blurb: 'Small, quiet, cheap ice makers that suit dorm rules and tiny counters.',
    tab: 'Best Ice Maker for a Dorm Room (2026): Small, Quiet, Cheap',
    summary: 'Best ice makers for dorm rooms: compact bullet and nugget machines compared on size, noise and price, plus housing-rule checks, power, and how to store ice.',
    heading: 'Best ice maker for a dorm room',
    lede: 'A dorm ice maker needs to be small, simple, plug-and-play, and allowed by your housing rules. Most are countertop machines that make a few pounds of ice an hour and store it only briefly.',
    quick: 'For most students, a Frigidaire 26 lb countertop ice maker is the best dorm pick: it is cheap, small, and makes bullet ice in minutes. If you want soft nugget ice, the Frigidaire Gallery 33 lb or the Euhomy nugget maker cost far less than an Opal, and the narrow Kismile fits the tightest shelf. Check your residence hall appliance rules before you buy.',
    list: ['Frigidaire 26 lb Countertop Ice Maker', 'Igloo 26 lb Automatic Ice Maker', 'Euhomy Countertop Ice Maker (26.5 lb)', 'Kismile Nugget Ice Maker (35 lb)', 'Euhomy Nugget Ice Maker (34 lb)', 'Frigidaire Gallery Nugget Ice Maker (33 lb)'],
    parts: [
      ['Dorm picks compared', compare('Compact ice makers for dorms', ['frigidaire26', 'igloo26', 'euhomybullet', 'kismile', 'euhomynugget', 'frigidairegallery'], ['Ice', 'Daily output', 'Bin', 'Size', 'Price band'])],
      ['Check housing rules first', `<p>Residence halls set their own rules on appliances, and they differ from school to school. One large university lists refrigerators over 4.4 cubic feet, microwaves over 700 watts, appliances with open heating elements and space heaters among its prohibited items; countertop ice makers are not on that list, but your hall may differ. Look for an approved or prohibited appliance list on your housing office site and check the limits on wattage, outlets and extension cords. If an ice maker is not mentioned, ask the housing office before you buy. A refund after move-in is harder than a question in August.</p>`],
      ['Best cheap dorm pick', pick('frigidaire26', 'Best overall for dorms', 'The most popular dorm ice maker for a reason: it is cheap, light, and makes a batch of bullet ice in minutes. It does not keep ice frozen, so move finished ice to the freezer of your mini fridge.')],
      ['Best with a handle', pick('igloo26', 'Best for moving', 'The Igloo has a carry handle on some versions, a self-clean mode and retro colors. That makes it easy to move between a dorm and a summer home. It makes bullet ice only.')],
      ['Best narrow nugget maker', pick('kismile', 'Best for tight spaces', 'About 9 inches wide, so it slides onto a crowded shelf or a desk corner. The small water tank means you will refill often, and the build is basic, but it is one of the cheapest ways to get nugget ice.')],
      ['Best value nugget ice', pick('euhomynugget', 'Best nugget for the money', 'Nugget ice at about a third of an Opal price, with a fast first batch and a size that suits a dorm counter. Louder and less polished than an Opal, which matters in a shared room.')],
      ['Best big-brand nugget', pick('frigidairegallery', 'Best brand-name nugget', 'Touch controls, a window and self-clean from a household name, usually for less than an Opal. A good choice for a student who wants nugget ice and may want to bring it to an apartment later.')],
      ['Size, noise and power', `<ul><li><b>Footprint.</b> Bullet ice makers are about 12 by 11 by 9 inches. Nugget makers are taller; the Kismile is about 9 inches wide. Measure your counter, shelf or desk, including the lid swing.</li><li><b>Noise.</b> Ice makers cycle on and off, and nugget machines can be loud. Put it where a roommate will not hear it at night, and consider turning it off when you sleep.</li><li><b>Power.</b> Countertop ice makers draw modest power compared with a microwave. Manufacturers list roughly 115 to 190 watts on some models. Use a wall outlet or a surge protector, not a long thin extension cord.</li><li><b>Water.</b> Most are manual fill: you pour water into a reservoir. Keep a bottle nearby. Some can connect to a water line, which dorms rarely allow.</li></ul>`],
      ['How much ice you actually get', `<p>A 26 lb a day rating sounds like a lot, but a countertop machine holds only 1.5 to 3 pounds at a time. It stops when the basket is full, and it makes more as you use ice. That is enough for drinks and a small cooler, not for a party. For game days, make ice ahead and move it to a freezer bag. The ratings are measured in ideal conditions, and a warm room lowers real output.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>What to expect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Daily drinks for one or two people</td><td>A countertop machine keeps up easily</td></tr><tr><td>Small party of 6 to 10</td><td>Start a day ahead and keep a freezer bag of ice</td></tr><tr><td>Party of 20 or more</td><td>Buy bagged ice. A countertop machine cannot keep up.</td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['Storing ice in a dorm', `<p>Countertop ice makers keep ice cold but not frozen. The ice slowly melts and the water returns to the reservoir to be remade. That is normal, but it means you cannot keep a week of ice on the counter. To store ice, scoop it into a freezer-safe bag and put it in your mini fridge freezer. Do not leave a machine running unattended for days, and empty the water before you leave for a break.</p>`],
      ['Cleaning and move-out', `<p>Clean the ice maker every few weeks, more often in hard water, and sanitize before storing it for summer. For step-by-step cleaning, see <a href="/how-to-clean-portable-ice-maker/">how to clean a portable ice maker</a>. Empty and dry it completely before packing it, and leave the lid open so odors do not build up. Mold in a closed, damp machine is the most common reason students throw one away after summer.</p>`],
      ['Bullet or nugget in a dorm', `<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Bullet</th><th>Nugget</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Price</td><td>Usually under $100</td><td>Starts at about $120 for budget brands</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>First batch in minutes</td><td>First batch in minutes to about 20 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Noise</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Often louder</td></tr><tr><td>Taste and texture</td><td>Hard, cloudy</td><td>Soft, chewable</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Cold drinks, coolers</td><td>Chewing, blended drinks, soda</td></tr></tbody></table><p>See <a href="/what-is-nugget-ice/">what nugget ice is</a> if you are not sure which you want. If you are choosing for yourself and a roommate, pick bullet for the budget and nugget for the experience.</p>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['Are ice makers allowed in dorms?', 'It depends on your school. Many halls allow small countertop appliances, but some limit wattage or ban certain items. Check your housing office list and ask if the ice maker is not mentioned.'],
      ['What is the best cheap ice maker for a dorm?', 'The Frigidaire 26 lb countertop ice maker is the most common choice because it is cheap and small. The Igloo and Euhomy bullet makers are similar.'],
      ['Can a dorm ice maker keep ice frozen?', 'No. Countertop machines keep ice cold for a few hours but not frozen. Move ice to a freezer bag if you want to store it.'],
      ['Is nugget ice worth it in a dorm?', 'If you chew ice or drink a lot of soda and iced coffee, yes. Budget nugget makers cost much less than an Opal, but they are louder.'],
    ],
    refs: [['ecozy, nugget ice makers', 'https://ecozy.com/collections/nugget-ice-maker'], ['Texas A&M Residence Life, appliances and equipment policy', 'https://reslife.tamu.edu/on-campus-housing-policies/appliances-c/'], ['Costway, nugget ice maker countertop', 'https://www.costway.com/nugget-ice-maker-countertop-with-soft-chewable-pellet.html']],
    next: ['best-small-ice-maker', 'best-portable-ice-maker', 'best-cheap-nugget-ice-maker', 'best-countertop-ice-maker', 'how-to-clean-portable-ice-maker'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'best-freestanding-ice-maker', short: 'Freestanding ice makers', blurb: 'Stand-alone ice makers for homes, garages and bars, with or without a drain.',
    tab: 'Best Freestanding Ice Maker for Home (2026): Picks and Buying Guide',
    summary: 'Best freestanding ice makers for home: floor and counter units compared on output, bin size, drain and water needs, with picks for bars, garages and kitchens.',
    heading: 'Best freestanding ice maker for home',
    lede: 'A freestanding ice maker sits on its own, so you do not need a cabinet cutout. Some sit on the floor, some on the counter, and the best choice depends on how much ice you need and whether you can run water and a drain.',
    quick: 'For a floor-standing machine that can also be built in, the Whynter UIM-502SS (up to 80 lb of clear cubes a day, 25 lb bin) is the best all-rounder, and the Euhomy 100 lb undercounter is the best high-output value. For a freestanding unit that needs no drain, choose the Whynter UIM-155 (12 lb a day), and for a counter, the Frigidaire 26 lb machine.',
    list: ['Whynter UIM-502SS', 'Euhomy 100 lb Undercounter Ice Maker', 'Whynter FIM-450HS', 'Whynter UIM-155', 'Frigidaire 26 lb Countertop Ice Maker'],
    parts: [
      ['Freestanding picks at a glance', compare('Freestanding ice makers compared', ['whynter502', 'euhomy100', 'whynterfim450', 'whynter155', 'frigidaire26'], ['Ice', 'Daily output', 'Bin', 'Price band'])],
      ['Best overall', pick('whynter502', 'Best overall freestanding', 'Whynter rates this 15 inch unit up to 80 pounds of clear cubes a day with a 25 lb bin. It runs freestanding or built in, ships with an NSF inline filter, a scoop and hoses, and is listed as ENERGY STAR. It is 14.6 inches wide and 32.8 inches tall.')],
      ['Best for high output', pick('euhomy100', 'Best high-capacity value', 'Up to 100 pounds a day and a 33 lb bin in a 15 inch cabinet. Best for entertaining or a small business. It needs a drain or a pump version.')],
      ['Best plumbed bullet ice', pick('whynterfim450', 'Best direct-plumbed freestanding', 'Hooked to a water line, this freestanding unit makes about 44 pounds of bullet ice a day, so you never refill a tank. It is 15 inches wide and 23 inches tall, small enough for a garage or den.')],
      ['Best with no drain', pick('whynter155', 'Best no-drain freestanding', 'Up to 12 pounds of crescent ice a day, a 6 lb bin and no drain needed. It works freestanding or built in, with a reversible door, a 25 foot water line hose, and a one-year warranty. Output is low, so it suits couples and small bars.')],
      ['Best on a counter', pick('frigidaire26', 'Best budget freestanding', 'A countertop machine is the simplest freestanding option: set it down, fill the tank, and press a button. It makes bullet ice in minutes, and you can take it anywhere.')],
      ['Freestanding, built-in or countertop', `<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Countertop</th><th>Freestanding floor unit</th><th>Built-in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Install</td><td>Plug in, fill the tank</td><td>Place, plug in, connect water and drain</td><td>Cabinet cutout, vented front</td></tr><tr><td>Output</td><td>About 26 to 40 lb a day</td><td>About 12 to 100 lb a day</td><td>Same range as freestanding</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>Small basket, ice melts</td><td>6 to 33 lb insulated bin</td><td>Same as freestanding</td></tr><tr><td>Moves easily</td><td>Yes</td><td>With help</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Many 15 inch units, including the Whynter and Euhomy models above, can be freestanding or built in. The difference is the vents: units made to be built in usually vent from the front. Check the manual before you box one into a cabinet.</p>`],
      ['Water and drain needs', `<ul><li><b>Manual fill.</b> You pour water into a reservoir. No plumbing, but frequent refills.</li><li><b>Water line.</b> Direct plumbing gives you a constant supply. Whynter recommends a licensed plumber for installation.</li><li><b>Drain.</b> Most clear cube units drain melt water. Check whether the unit drains by gravity, has a pump, or is drainless.</li><li><b>No drain at all.</b> The UIM-155 needs no drain; melt water is recycled.</li></ul>`],
      ['Where it can go', `<p>Freestanding units can sit in garages, bars, basements and patios, but each has an ambient temperature limit. Units made for indoor use should not sit in direct sun or an unheated space. Leave a few inches of space at the vents, keep the floor level, and place it near the water line. Weight matters on upper floors: a full bin adds pounds, and a compact unit can weigh over 40 pounds empty (Whynter lists the UIM-155 at about 42 pounds net).</p>`],
      ['Maintenance', `<p>Clean the unit and bin on the schedule in the manual, replace the filter, and keep the vents free of dust. See <a href="/how-to-clean-portable-ice-maker/">how to clean a portable ice maker</a> and <a href="/best-ice-maker-cleaning-solution/">best ice maker cleaners</a>. Manual-defrost units need you to turn them off, empty them and let them dry now and then.</p>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['What is a freestanding ice maker?', 'A freestanding ice maker is a stand-alone machine that does not need to be built into cabinetry. Countertop machines and floor-standing 15 inch units both count.'],
      ['Can a freestanding ice maker be built in?', 'Some can. Whynter lists the UIM-502SS and UIM-155 as both freestanding and built-in. Check the manual for vent and clearance rules.'],
      ['Do freestanding ice makers need a drain?', 'Many do, but not all. The Whynter UIM-155 needs no drain, while the UIM-502SS ships with a drain hose.'],
      ['How much ice does a freestanding ice maker make?', 'Anywhere from about 12 pounds to 100 pounds a day, depending on the model.'],
    ],
    refs: [['Whynter, UIM-502SS built-in ice maker', 'https://www.whynter.com/product/uim-502ss/'], ['Whynter, FIM-450HS freestanding ice maker', 'https://www.whynter.com/product/fim-450hs/'], ['Whynter, UIM-155 built-in ice maker', 'https://www.whynter.com/product/uim-155/'], ['GoFoodservice, undercounter ice machine buying guide', 'https://www.gofoodservice.com/guides/undercounter-ice-machine-buying-guide']],
    next: ['best-undercounter-ice-maker', 'best-ice-maker-for-home-bar', 'best-countertop-ice-maker', 'best-portable-ice-maker', 'best-large-capacity-ice-maker'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'best-12v-ice-maker', short: 'Ice makers for cars and boats', blurb: 'What really runs on 12V: battery, inverter and marine ice makers compared.',
    tab: 'Best 12V Ice Maker for Cars and Boats: What Actually Works',
    summary: 'Best ice makers for a car, boat or RV: which run on 12V, which need an inverter, how much power they draw, and how heat cuts output.',
    heading: 'Best 12V ice maker for cars and boats',
    lede: 'True 12 volt DC ice makers are rare. Most machines sold for cars, boats and RVs either run on 120 volt AC through an inverter or on a dedicated battery. Here is what actually works and how to power it.',
    quick: 'For a car or truck, the Silonn 4-power ice maker is the pick that is listed to run directly from a 12V/24V car power supply, a battery, solar or a wall outlet. For a boat with shore power or a generator, the Maxx Ice marine machine (115 V AC, 25 lb a day at test conditions, 12 lb bin) is built for the job. A standard countertop ice maker plus an inverter and a portable power station is the flexible alternative.',
    list: ['Silonn Portable 4-Power Battery Ice Maker', 'Maxx Ice MMAR25B Compact Marine Ice Machine', 'Frigidaire 26 lb Countertop Ice Maker (with inverter)', 'Igloo 26 lb Automatic Ice Maker (with inverter)', 'Euhomy Countertop Ice Maker (26.5 lb) (with inverter)'],
    parts: [
      ['Picks at a glance', `<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Ice</th><th>Rated output</th><th>Power it needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>${nameLink('silonnbattery')}</td><td>Bullet</td><td>Up to 22 lb a day</td><td>Battery, solar, 12V/24V car power or AC</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('maxxmarine')}</td><td>Crescent</td><td>25.1 lb a day at 70 F air and 50 F water; 12.9 lb at 90 F and 70 F</td><td>115 V AC</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('igloo26')}</td><td>Bullet</td><td>Up to 26 lb a day</td><td>AC (inverter or power station)</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('frigidaire26')}</td><td>Bullet</td><td>Up to 26 lb a day</td><td>AC (inverter or power station)</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('euhomybullet')}</td><td>Bullet</td><td>Up to 26.5 lb a day</td><td>AC (inverter or power station)</td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['The 12V reality check', `<p>Ice makers use a compressor, a pump and heaters. Most are built for a 120 volt household outlet. Listings that say 12V often mean one of three things:</p><ul><li><b>A true 12V or battery model.</b> Rare. The Silonn 4-power machine is listed to run from a battery, solar, a 12V/24V car supply or 100 to 240 V AC.</li><li><b>A 120 V AC machine</b> that needs an inverter or a power station. This is the usual setup in boats and vans.</li><li><b>A 12V cooler or fridge</b> that keeps ice cold but does not make much ice.</li></ul><p>Read the power spec on the listing and the nameplate, not just the title. A marine ice maker is not automatically a 12V machine: the Maxx Ice MMAR25 is a 115 V AC unit.</p>`],
      ['Best for cars, trucks and campers', pick('silonnbattery', 'Best 12V-capable ice maker', 'Silonn lists a 4-way power system: an insertable rechargeable battery (sold separately), solar, a 12V/24V car power supply or a standard 100 to 240 V AC outlet. It makes nine bullet-shaped cubes in about 6 minutes, at up to 22 pounds a day. Treat the daily number as an upper limit, and check the exact power accessories in the listing before you buy.')],
      ['Best for boats', pick('maxxmarine', 'Best marine ice maker', 'Built for boats, with a travel latch that keeps the door closed underway, overflow prevention and no drain connection needed. It is a 115 V AC unit, so it needs shore power, a generator or an inverter. It draws 6.4 amps normally and 11.3 amps at most at 120 V, and weighs 93 pounds.')],
      ['Best cheap AC option with an inverter', pick('igloo26', 'Best budget pick for an inverter setup', 'A light countertop machine with a carry handle on some versions makes bullet ice in minutes. Run it from a pure sine inverter or a portable power station, and move the ice to a cooler.')],
      ['Heat cuts output hard', `<p>Rated daily output assumes cool conditions. Maxx lists its marine machine at 25.1 pounds a day with 70 F air and 50 F water, but only 12.9 pounds a day with 90 F air and 70 F water. That is roughly half. A hot boat cabin, a sun-baked van or a warm lake water supply will cut output the same way. Plan for half the rated output in summer, and shade the machine.</p>`],
      ['How much power does it use', `<p>One review of portable ice makers puts the average at about 100 watts while running, with 200 watts or more at start-up, and a batch takes 7 to 15 minutes. Your model will differ, so check its label. Here is the math for sizing power:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Running power</td><td>about 100 W (check your label)</td></tr><tr><td>Battery current through an inverter</td><td>100 W / (12 V x 0.85 efficiency) = about 9.8 A. The 0.85 is an assumed inverter efficiency</td></tr><tr><td>Start-up surge</td><td>200 W or more = about 20 A or more</td></tr><tr><td>Energy per 15 minute batch</td><td>100 W x 0.25 h = 25 Wh</td></tr><tr><td>Batches from a 600 Wh power station</td><td>about 24 (600 / 25), before losses</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Choose an inverter or power station rated well above the start-up draw. Use an inverter with a pure sine wave output when the manual calls for it, and keep cable runs short and thick so voltage does not sag.</p>`],
      ['Car use: what to know', `<ul><li><b>Engine off.</b> Do not run an ice maker from the starter battery with the engine off. Use a separate battery or a portable power station.</li><li><b>Socket limits.</b> A cigarette lighter socket is fused for a limited current. Check your vehicle manual before plugging in anything that draws a surge.</li><li><b>Level ground.</b> Manufacturers tell you to level a unit front to back and side to side. A tilted car or van can stop ice production.</li><li><b>Fill and carry.</b> Most portable machines are manual fill. Carry water in a jug.</li><li><b>Where the ice goes.</b> These machines do not keep ice frozen. Scoop it into a cooler or a 12V fridge as it is made.</li></ul>`],
      ['Boat use: what to know', `<ul><li><b>Power.</b> Plan for 115 V AC from shore power, a generator or a big inverter. Add up the amps of everything running at once.</li><li><b>Mounting.</b> Use a unit with a travel latch and overflow prevention, and secure it so it cannot shift. The Maxx marine machine includes a removable flange for built-in installation.</li><li><b>Drain.</b> The Maxx unit needs no drain connection. Many others need to drain melt water somewhere.</li><li><b>Water.</b> Use fresh potable water, filtered if possible. Never feed an ice maker salt water.</li><li><b>Humidity and salt.</b> Wipe the unit and dry the interior after use. The Maxx includes a door prop for air-drying the inside when powered off.</li></ul>`],
      ['RVs and camping', `<p>RVs often have a 120 V outlet at the campsite or an inverter on board, which makes a standard countertop ice maker practical. For more on that setup, see <a href="/ice-maker-for-camping-and-rv/">ice makers for camping and RVs</a> and <a href="/best-portable-ice-maker/">portable ice makers</a>.</p>`],
      ['Alternatives to making ice on the road', `<ul><li><b>Block ice or bagged ice in a cooler.</b> Cheap and silent.</li><li><b>A 12V compressor fridge.</b> Cools drinks and food without ice.</li><li><b>Silicone trays in a 12V freezer.</b> Slow, but efficient on power.</li></ul><p>An ice maker is most useful if you need drinks over ice every day and have solid power. For a weekend trip, a cooler and a bag of ice may be simpler.</p>`],
      ['Buying checklist', `<ol><li>Confirm the exact power input: 12V DC, battery, 120 V AC, or several.</li><li>Check running watts and start-up watts on the label.</li><li>Size the inverter or power station for the start-up draw.</li><li>Check the output rating conditions, and halve the rating in summer.</li><li>Check size, weight, and mounting.</li><li>Check drain and water needs.</li></ol>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['Is there a real 12V ice maker?', 'There are a few. The Silonn 4-power ice maker is listed to run from a 12V/24V car supply, a rechargeable battery, solar or AC. Most other ice makers are 120 V AC.'],
      ['Can I run an ice maker from my car?', 'Yes, with a compatible model or an inverter, but do not run it from the starter battery with the engine off. A portable power station is safer.'],
      ['How many watts does an ice maker use?', 'Portable ice makers average around 100 watts while running and can draw 200 watts or more at start-up. Check your label.'],
      ['Will a marine ice maker run on 12V?', 'Not necessarily. The Maxx Ice MMAR25 marine machine runs on 115 V AC.'],
      ['Why does my ice maker make less ice in summer?', 'Output ratings assume cool air and water. Maxx lists its marine unit at 25.1 pounds a day at 70 F air and 50 F water and 12.9 pounds at 90 F air and 70 F water.'],
    ],
    refs: [['Maxx Ice, compact marine ice machine specifications (via dealer listing)', 'https://www.chefsdeal.com/maxximum-mmar25w-cube-style-ice-maker-with-bin.html'], ['Jackery, how many watts ice makers use', 'https://www.jackery.com/blogs/buying-advice/solar-powered-ice-maker'], ['Silonn 4-power ice maker, features and power options', 'https://ownpetz.com/blog/article/silonn-portable-4-power-ice-maker-review-b5671'], ['GoFoodservice, ice machine installation checklist', 'https://www.gofoodservice.com/guides/undercounter-ice-machine-buying-guide']],
    next: ['ice-maker-for-camping-and-rv', 'best-portable-ice-maker', 'best-small-ice-maker', 'best-countertop-ice-maker', 'best-freestanding-ice-maker'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'silonn-ice-maker-review', short: 'Silonn ice maker review', blurb: 'Silonn\'s cheap bullet ice maker and its nugget, clear and sphere models.', review: { name: 'Silonn Countertop Ice Maker (26 lb)', brand: 'Silonn', score: 7.3 },
    tab: 'Silonn Countertop Ice Maker Review (2026): Worth Buying?',
    summary: 'Silonn countertop ice maker review: the 26 lb bullet model, its size and power, how it compares with Frigidaire and Igloo, plus Silonn nugget and clear makers.',
    heading: 'Silonn countertop ice maker review',
    lede: 'Silonn sells a popular budget bullet ice maker, and it has added nugget, clear cube, sphere and battery-powered machines. Here is what the main model offers and who should buy it.',
    quick: 'The Silonn countertop ice maker is a good low-price bullet ice maker: it lists 9 cubes in about 6 minutes, up to 26 pounds a day, a narrow 8.7 inch body, self-cleaning and two cube sizes. It does not keep ice frozen, and the basket is small. Our rating: 7.3 out of 10. Choose it for a kitchen, dorm or office, and look at nugget or clear models if you want something fancier.',
    list: ['Silonn Countertop Ice Maker (26 lb, SLIM01)', 'Frigidaire 26 lb Countertop Ice Maker', 'Igloo 26 lb Automatic Ice Maker', 'Silonn Clear Ice Cube Maker Countertop'],
    parts: [
      ['The verdict', pick('silonnbullet', 'Our rating: 7.3 out of 10', 'A fast, compact, low-price bullet ice maker with a self-clean mode. It is a good fit for a small kitchen, dorm or office, and a poor fit for anyone who wants ice for a crowd or ice that stays frozen.')],
      ['Specs at a glance', `<table><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>Silonn countertop ice maker</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ice type</td><td>Bullet, two sizes</td></tr><tr><td>Rated output</td><td>Up to 26 lb in 24 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Cycle</td><td>9 cubes in about 6 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Size (D x W x H)</td><td>12.3 x 8.7 x 12.5 in</td></tr><tr><td>Power</td><td>115 V, 150 W</td></tr><tr><td>Water tank</td><td>2 L</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaning</td><td>Self-cleaning mode</td></tr><tr><td>Included</td><td>Ice scoop and basket</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 8.7 inch width helps on a crowded counter. The 150 watt rating is typical for a countertop compressor machine.</p>`],
      ['How it compares', `<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Ice</th><th>Rated output</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>${nameLink('silonnbullet')}</td><td>Bullet</td><td>26 lb a day</td><td>8.7 in wide, 2 L tank</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('frigidaire26')}</td><td>Bullet</td><td>26 lb a day</td><td>About 12 x 11 x 9 in, big-brand name</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('igloo26')}</td><td>Bullet</td><td>26 lb a day</td><td>Carry handle on some versions, retro colors</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('euhomybullet')}</td><td>Bullet</td><td>26.5 lb a day</td><td>Light, with a handle</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On paper these four are nearly the same. Differences come down to size, controls, warranty and the brand you trust. Pick the one that fits your counter and is cheapest at checkout. For the full range see <a href="/best-countertop-ice-maker/">best countertop ice makers</a>.</p>`],
      ['What the 26 lb rating means', `<p>Daily output ratings assume ideal conditions: cool room, cool water, and a machine that runs nonstop. In a warm kitchen, output is lower. For a sense of scale, Maxx lists one compact machine at 25.1 pounds a day in 70 F air and 50 F water, but only 12.9 pounds a day in 90 F air and 70 F water. Treat the 26 lb figure as a ceiling, and expect real output to be lower in summer. The basket also limits how much ice sits ready, because the machine stops when the basket is full.</p>`],
      ['Who should buy it', `<ul><li>Students and renters who want cold drinks on demand and have limited space.</li><li>Office break rooms and home bars that need a few batches a day.</li><li>Anyone who wants a cheap second ice machine for cookouts and coolers.</li></ul>`],
      ['Who should skip it', `<ul><li>Chewable-ice fans. Bullet ice is hard; see <a href="/best-nugget-ice-maker/">nugget ice makers</a>.</li><li>Cocktail drinkers who want clear cubes. Silonn sells a separate clear ice maker for that.</li><li>Large parties. A basket machine cannot keep pace, so buy bagged ice or an undercounter machine.</li><li>Anyone who wants to store ice. The machine keeps ice cold for a few hours, not frozen.</li></ul>`],
      ['The rest of the Silonn lineup', `<table><thead><tr><th>Silonn model</th><th>Ice</th><th>Details from the maker</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>${nameLink('silonnclear')}</td><td>Clear cubes</td><td>1.6 in cubes, 3 in about 20 minutes, about 26 lb a day, 3 thickness settings</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('silonnbattery')}</td><td>Bullet</td><td>Battery, solar, 12V/24V car or AC power; up to 22 lb a day</td></tr><tr><td>Silonn nugget ice maker</td><td>Nugget</td><td>Countertop nugget machine with app and voice control</td></tr><tr><td>Silonn sphere ice maker</td><td>Sphere</td><td>2.4 in spheres, 3 in about 40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['Setup and care', `<ol><li>Place it on a level surface with a few inches of room around it.</li><li>Rinse the tank and basket, and run a cleaning cycle before first use.</li><li>Fill the tank to the marked line with fresh water. Using filtered water slows scale.</li><li>Press the ice button. Most countertop models have indicators for low water and a full basket; check your manual for how the Silonn shows them.</li><li>Scoop ice out and move it to a freezer bag if you want to keep it.</li><li>Empty the water if you will not use it for a few days, and leave the lid open to dry.</li></ol><p>For cleaning steps, see <a href="/how-to-clean-portable-ice-maker/">how to clean a portable ice maker</a>. Where the Silonn manual differs, follow it.</p>`],
      ['Troubleshooting', `<ul><li><b>No ice, pump running.</b> Check the water level, and let the machine rest if it just ran a cycle.</li><li><b>Ice is small or hollow.</b> Often water that is too warm or a room that is too hot. Use cold water and move the machine to a cooler spot.</li><li><b>Cloudy or off-tasting ice.</b> Descale, rinse well and use filtered water.</li><li><b>Basket full light stays on.</b> Empty the basket and check that the sensor area is clean and dry.</li></ul>`],
      ['How bullet ice makers work', `<p>A countertop bullet ice maker pumps water from the tank over a cold metal evaporator with small cylinders. Ice forms on the cylinders in a few minutes, then the machine warms the evaporator briefly so the cubes drop into the basket. It repeats this until the basket is full. Because the basket is not a freezer, ice slowly melts, and the melt water drains back to the tank to be used again. That is why the water tank shrinks as you use the machine, and why you should not leave ice in the basket for days. If you want more detail, see <a href="/countertop-ice-maker-that-keeps-ice-frozen/">why countertop ice makers do not keep ice frozen</a>.</p>`],
      ['What you will actually use it for', `<table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>How the Silonn fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Iced coffee and tea each morning</td><td>Easily. One or two batches covers a household.</td></tr><tr><td>Water bottles and a small cooler</td><td>Works. Run it a couple of hours ahead and bag the ice.</td></tr><tr><td>A cookout for 10</td><td>Marginal. Start the day before and store ice in the freezer.</td></tr><tr><td>A party for 25 or more</td><td>Not enough. Buy bagged ice.</td></tr><tr><td>Chewable ice</td><td>Not the right type</td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['Cost of ownership', `<p>The machine is cheap; ongoing costs are small. It draws about 150 watts while running, so even running it a few hours a day adds little to a power bill. The costs that matter are water (use filtered water if yours is hard), cleaning supplies such as a descaler made for ice makers (see <a href="/best-ice-maker-cleaning-solution/">best ice maker cleaners</a>), and the machine's lifespan. Budget countertop machines are consumer appliances, and many owners treat them as a few-year purchase. Check the warranty terms on the listing before you buy.</p>`],
      ['Choosing between look-alikes', `<p>Many brands sell near-identical bullet machines, including Euhomy, Cowsar, Igloo and Frigidaire. They share the same basic design and the same 26 pound rating. Compare these things before you decide: width and height for your counter, cleaning mode and how easy the basket and tank are to remove, the warranty length and who handles it, and recent owner feedback about noise and reliability. If price is the same, a brand with a stronger service network is the safer bet.</p>`],
      ['Verdict', `<p>If you want inexpensive bullet ice and your counter is small, the Silonn does the job and the self-clean mode keeps upkeep simple. If you want soft ice or large cubes, buy a different machine.</p>${buy('silonnbullet', 'Check Silonn price on Amazon')}`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['How much ice does the Silonn countertop ice maker make?', 'Silonn lists up to 26 pounds in 24 hours and 9 cubes in about 6 minutes. Real output depends on room and water temperature.'],
      ['Does the Silonn ice maker keep ice frozen?', 'No. Like all countertop machines it keeps ice cold briefly, and the ice melts. Move it to a freezer if you want to store it.'],
      ['How big is the Silonn countertop ice maker?', 'The listing gives 12.3 inches deep, 8.7 inches wide and 12.5 inches tall, and 150 watts at 115 volts.'],
      ['Does Silonn make nugget ice?', 'Yes. Silonn sells a separate countertop nugget ice maker with app and voice control, as well as clear cube, sphere and battery-powered models.'],
      ['Is the Silonn ice maker self-cleaning?', 'Yes. It has a self-cleaning mode, but you should still empty the tank and wipe the interior regularly.'],
    ],
    refs: [['Amazon product details, Silonn SLIM01B (dimensions, wattage, capacity)', 'https://www.amazon.com/clp/B08ZYJ8CRX'], ['Silonn, clear ice cube maker countertop', 'https://www.silonn.com/products/silonn-clear-ice-cube-maker-countertop'], ['Silonn, home page (sphere and nugget models)', 'https://www.silonn.com/'], ['Maxx Ice, marine ice machine specifications (rating conditions)', 'https://www.chefsdeal.com/maxximum-mmar25w-cube-style-ice-maker-with-bin.html']],
    next: ['best-countertop-ice-maker', 'best-portable-ice-maker', 'best-small-ice-maker', 'how-to-clean-portable-ice-maker', 'igloo-countertop-ice-maker-review', 'euhomy-countertop-ice-maker-review'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'ecozy-nugget-ice-maker-review', short: 'ecozy nugget review', blurb: 'ecozy\'s smart nugget ice maker and dispenser, compared with the Opal.', review: { name: 'ecozy Smart Nugget Ice Maker (IM-NS451B)', brand: 'ecozy', score: 8.0 },
    tab: 'ecozy Nugget Ice Maker Review (2026): 451B and Dispenser',
    summary: 'ecozy nugget ice maker review: the smart 451B and the IM-ND481B dispenser, with specs, TDS monitor, app, cleaning, and how they compare with the GE Opal.',
    heading: 'ecozy nugget ice maker review',
    lede: 'ecozy has built a wide nugget lineup, from a smart 44 lb countertop machine to a touchless dispenser and an undercounter model. Here is how the main machines stack up.',
    quick: 'The ecozy Smart Nugget Ice Maker (IM-NS451B) is a strong Opal alternative: up to 44 pounds a day, a smart panel with a water-quality (TDS) monitor, app and voice control, and a timer, usually for less than a GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra. The dispenser (IM-ND481B) adds touchless ice and water but is large and louder. Our rating for the 451B: 8.0 out of 10.',
    list: ['ecozy Smart Nugget Ice Maker (IM-NS451B)', 'ecozy Nugget Ice Maker Dispenser (IM-ND481B)', 'GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra', 'Euhomy Nugget Ice Maker (34 lb)', 'Frigidaire Gallery Nugget Ice Maker (33 lb)'],
    parts: [
      ['The verdict', pick('ecozy451', 'Our rating: 8.0 out of 10', 'A well-equipped smart nugget maker with high rated output and a water-quality sensor, at a price below an Opal. Best for buyers who want features and nugget ice without Opal pricing.')],
      ['The ecozy lineup', `<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Type</th><th>Key details</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>IM-NS451B Smart Nugget</td><td>Countertop</td><td>Up to 44 lb a day, 192 W, smart panel, TDS monitor, app</td></tr><tr><td>IM-NS462B Mistalo Max</td><td>Countertop</td><td>Smart nugget model; check the listing for output and features</td></tr><tr><td>IM-NS280C Mistalo</td><td>Countertop</td><td>Countertop nugget model listed as crunchy, chewable ice</td></tr><tr><td>IM-ND481B Dispenser</td><td>Countertop dispenser</td><td>Touchless ice dispensing, water dispensing, app</td></tr><tr><td>IM-ND332C Mistalo Drop Slim</td><td>Countertop dispenser</td><td>Slimmer nugget dispenser</td></tr><tr><td>IM-NB661A</td><td>Undercounter</td><td>Up to 66 lb of nugget ice a day</td></tr></tbody></table><p>ecozy also sells bullet, square cube and sphere ice makers, including 2 inch and 2.4 inch clear ice ball makers. Check the model number before you buy, because the names are close.</p>`],
      ['What the 451B offers', `<ul><li><b>Output.</b> ecozy rates it up to 44 lb a day, and lists 192 watts.</li><li><b>Smart panel.</b> A full-color touch panel shows the time, air and water temperature and TDS, with buttons for WiFi, self-clean, timer, interior light and start/stop.</li><li><b>TDS monitor.</b> The sensor warns when water quality exceeds 200 ppm (parts per million of dissolved solids). ecozy recommends bottled or purified water, which gives clearer ice and less scale.</li><li><b>App and voice.</b> You can start or stop ice and schedule production from the EcozyHome app, Alexa or Google Home.</li><li><b>Insulated window.</b> A double-layer insulated flip door with a see-through window keeps ice colder for longer than a single layer.</li><li><b>Self-clean.</b> A one-touch clean cycle takes about 20 minutes. Run it before first use.</li><li><b>Removable parts.</b> The tank and basket come out for deep cleaning, and the tank has a flip lid for filling while it is in place.</li></ul>`],
      ['Dispenser model', pick('ecozydispenser', 'Best for touchless ice and water', 'This countertop machine makes nugget ice, dispenses it and also dispenses water. It is large for a countertop appliance: about 19 by 11 by 16 inches and 42 pounds. ecozy says to leave 6 inches or more at each side for airflow.')],
      ['Using the dispenser', `<ul><li>First ice arrives in about 10 minutes, and filling the basket takes 30 minutes or more.</li><li>It stops automatically when the basket is full or the tank is empty.</li><li>The clean cycle runs about 5 minutes. After it finishes you drain the water through the hose at the back.</li><li>The manual recommends cleaning weekly if your water is below 120 TDS and every 3 days if it is above 120 TDS. The display shows your TDS reading.</li><li>The touchless dispensing is the draw. Owners also report the dispenser is loud for a countertop appliance.</li></ul>`],
      ['ecozy vs the Opal and budget rivals', `<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Rated output</th><th>Notable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>${nameLink('ecozy451')}</td><td>44 lb a day</td><td>TDS monitor, app, timer</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('opalultra')}</td><td>38 lb a day, 3 lb bin</td><td>GE brand, side tank, scale-inhibiting filter, large owner base</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('euhomynugget')}</td><td>34 lb a day</td><td>Cheap, fast first batch</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('frigidairegallery')}</td><td>33 lb a day</td><td>Big-brand, touch controls</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On specs, the ecozy leads on rated output and features. The GE Opal wins on brand support, a large owner community and GE's long-standing parts and service network. See our <a href="/ge-profile-opal-2-review/">GE Profile Opal 2.0 review</a> and <a href="/best-nugget-ice-maker/">best nugget ice makers</a>.</p>`],
      ['Water quality matters', `<p>Nugget machines scrape ice from an evaporator, so minerals in the water collect fast. A TDS meter helps you decide when to filter. ecozy suggests purified or bottled water for the best ice. If you will use tap water, a filter pitcher or a countertop purifier keeps TDS low and extends the time between descaling. For routines see <a href="/how-to-descale-ge-opal/">how to descale a nugget ice maker</a>, which uses the same principles, and always follow the ecozy manual for its own steps.</p>`],
      ['Who should buy an ecozy', `<ul><li>Nugget fans who want the most output for the price and like smart features.</li><li>Buyers who live with hard water and want to monitor it.</li><li>People who want a dispenser and have the counter space.</li></ul><p>Skip it if you want a long-established brand with local service, or if counter space is tight. Compare with the narrower <a href="/kismile-nugget-ice-maker-review/">Kismile</a> and the <a href="/euhomy-nugget-ice-maker-review/">Euhomy nugget ice maker</a>.</p>`],
      ['Setup in six steps', `<ol><li>Let the machine stand upright for a couple of hours after unpacking so the refrigerant settles.</li><li>Remove packing tape, film and foam from the basket and tray.</li><li>Wash the basket and tank with mild soap and water, and wipe the interior.</li><li>Run the one-touch clean cycle before the first batch.</li><li>Fill the tank with purified or filtered water to the max line and seat it fully.</li><li>Press start. The first ice drops in roughly ten minutes.</li></ol><p>Leave room at the sides and back for the fan, and keep the unit out of direct sun.</p>`],
      ['Common problems and fixes', `<table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Try this</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No ice or slow ice</td><td>Check the tank is seated, water is above the min line, and the room is not too hot</td></tr><tr><td>TDS warning</td><td>Switch to purified water and run a clean cycle</td></tr><tr><td>Ice tastes off</td><td>Clean the basket and tank, and replace the water</td></tr><tr><td>Loud running</td><td>Level the unit, give it airflow, and move it off a hollow counter</td></tr><tr><td>Water in the drip tray</td><td>Normal in humid rooms; empty it, and check for leaks if it recurs</td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['Running costs', `<p>The machine draws about 192 watts while running, so electricity is a small cost. The recurring costs are purified water or filters, and cleaning supplies. Budget for descaler and a plan to clean on the schedule in the manual. See <a href="/best-ice-maker-cleaning-solution/">best ice maker cleaners</a> for options.</p>`],
      ['Verdict', `<p>The ecozy 451B is one of the better-equipped nugget machines you can buy short of an Opal, and its water monitor is genuinely useful. Our rating is 8.0 out of 10, based on specs, manuals and owner feedback.</p>${buy('ecozy451', 'Check ecozy 451B price on Amazon')}`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['How much ice does the ecozy nugget ice maker make?', 'The IM-NS451B is rated up to 44 pounds a day. The undercounter IM-NB661A is rated up to 66 pounds a day.'],
      ['Is ecozy better than the GE Opal?', 'It offers more rated output and features for less money. The Opal has GE\'s service network and a very large owner base.'],
      ['Does the ecozy ice maker have an app?', 'Yes. The EcozyHome app starts and stops ice, sets timers and gives alerts, and the 451B also works with Alexa and Google Home.'],
      ['What water should I use in an ecozy ice maker?', 'ecozy recommends bottled or purified water. The 451B has a TDS sensor that notifies you when water exceeds 200 ppm.'],
      ['How often should I clean an ecozy ice maker?', 'For the dispenser, the manual says weekly if water is below 120 TDS and every 3 days if above 120 TDS. Check the manual for your model.'],
    ],
    refs: [['ecozy, nugget ice makers', 'https://ecozy.com/collections/nugget-ice-maker'], ['ecozy, ice maker lineup', 'https://ecozy.com/collections/ice-maker'], ['ecozy IM-NS451B product listing (capacity, wattage, dimensions)', 'https://www.amazon.com/clp/B0D9BBBDKV'], ['GE Appliances, Opal nugget ice makers', 'https://www.geappliances.com/ge/icemakers/opal-nugget-icemaker.htm']],
    next: ['best-nugget-ice-maker', 'ge-profile-opal-2-review', 'best-cheap-nugget-ice-maker', 'euhomy-nugget-ice-maker-review', 'how-to-clean-portable-ice-maker'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'vevor-commercial-ice-maker-review', short: 'Vevor commercial ice maker review', blurb: 'What Vevor\'s cheap commercial-style ice makers offer, and the ice crusher recall.', review: { name: 'VEVOR Commercial Ice Maker (130 lb)', brand: 'VEVOR', score: 7.0 },
    tab: 'Vevor Commercial Ice Maker Review (2026): Specs, Recall Check',
    summary: 'Vevor commercial ice maker review: 100 to 200 lb models, specs, drain and care, how they compare with Whynter and Hoshizaki, plus the Vevor ice crusher recall.',
    heading: 'Vevor commercial ice maker review',
    lede: 'Vevor sells large-capacity ice makers at prices well below restaurant brands. They are aimed at home bars, small cafes and food stands. Here is what the specs show, what to check before buying, and how the separate ice crusher recall fits in.',
    quick: 'Vevor commercial ice makers offer a lot of rated output for the money: the 130 lb model lists a 30 lb bin, 50 cubes in 8 to 10 minutes and ETL certification. They suit home bars and light commercial use, but not a busy restaurant that needs NSF listing and a service network. Our rating: 7.0 out of 10. The December 2025 Vevor recall covers BY-300 and BY-300FS ice crushers, not the ice makers.',
    list: ['VEVOR Commercial Ice Maker (130 lb, 30 lb bin)', 'Euhomy 100 lb Undercounter Ice Maker', 'Whynter UIM-502SS', 'Hoshizaki AM-50BAK'],
    parts: [
      ['Safety note: the Vevor recall', `<div class="note"><p><b>Recall check.</b> On December 11, 2025, the CPSC announced a recall of Vevor ice crushers (also sold as ice shavers and snow cone makers), models BY-300 and BY-300FS, because the machine can overheat and ignite. About 11,650 were sold in the U.S. from July 2021 through July 2025. Sanven reported two thermal events, one of them a fire. If you own one, stop using it and follow the refund steps on the CPSC notice. The recall lists no ice makers, and nothing on this page recommends a recalled product.</p></div>`],
      ['The verdict', pick('vevor130', 'Our rating: 7.0 out of 10', 'A high-output, low-price machine that works freestanding or under a counter, with adjustable cube thickness and a timer. It is a good value for a home bar or a small stand; a restaurant that needs commercial certification and local service should buy a commercial brand.')],
      ['Specs from the Vevor listing', `<table><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>130 lb model</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rated output</td><td>Up to 130 lb in 24 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Cycle</td><td>50 cubes in 8 to 10 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>30 lb maximum; ice-full light at 16 lb</td></tr><tr><td>Cube size</td><td>0.8 in (20 mm) square, thickness adjustable by freezing time</td></tr><tr><td>Insulation</td><td>Cyclopentane foam; keeps ice frozen 6 to 8 hours when powered off, per Vevor</td></tr><tr><td>Certification</td><td>ETL (electrical safety)</td></tr><tr><td>Placement</td><td>Freestanding or under counter, four adjustable feet</td></tr><tr><td>Controls</td><td>LED display with ambient temperature, timer, one-touch self-clean, full ice, low water and fault alerts</td></tr><tr><td>Setup</td><td>Inlet and drain hoses; keep upright 24 hours before use</td></tr></tbody></table><p>ETL shows electrical safety testing. It is not the same as NSF listing, which commercial kitchens commonly need. Check the listing and ask your health department if you run a food business.</p>`],
      ['The Vevor range', `<table><thead><tr><th>Model (from listings)</th><th>Rated output</th><th>Storage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>130 lb model</td><td>130 lb a day</td><td>30 lb</td></tr><tr><td>100 lb model</td><td>100 lb a day, 55 cubes in 12 to 15 minutes</td><td>33 lb</td></tr><tr><td>110 to 120 lb model</td><td>110 to 120 lb a day</td><td>19 lb</td></tr><tr><td>90 lb model</td><td>90 lb a day</td><td>12 lb</td></tr><tr><td>70 lb model</td><td>70 lb a day</td><td>11 lb</td></tr><tr><td>200 lb model</td><td>200 lb a day</td><td>25 to 45 lb</td></tr><tr><td>Undercounter nugget</td><td>66 lb a day</td><td>18 lb, with drain pump</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Vevor changes model names often. Match the storage and output you want against the exact listing you buy.</p>`],
      ['How it compares', `<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Rated output</th><th>Bin</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>${nameLink('vevor130')}</td><td>130 lb</td><td>30 lb</td><td>Freestanding or under counter, ETL</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('euhomy100')}</td><td>100 lb</td><td>33 lb</td><td>Similar class, ETL listed</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('whynter502')}</td><td>80 lb</td><td>25 lb</td><td>ENERGY STAR, NSF filter, scoop and hoses</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('hoshizakiam50bak')}</td><td>54 lb</td><td>27 lb</td><td>Commercial brand, 2 year parts and labor</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Vevor leads on rated output per dollar. The Hoshizaki wins on build, brand and warranty. For restaurants see <a href="/best-undercounter-commercial-ice-maker/">undercounter commercial ice makers</a>.</p>`],
      ['Install and clearance', `<ul><li>Leave 4 to 6 inches of space around the machine for airflow, away from ovens and direct sun.</li><li>Fit a water filter on the inlet line.</li><li>Run the drain hose to a proper outlet so melt water can drain.</li><li>Check that the power outlet matches the voltage in the manual.</li><li>Keep the unit upright for 24 hours before first use, as Vevor says.</li><li>Level it with the adjustable feet.</li></ul>`],
      ['Care schedule', `<table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>How often</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wipe the exterior with mild soap and water</td><td>Daily</td></tr><tr><td>Run the self-clean mode</td><td>Weekly</td></tr><tr><td>Descale if you have hard water</td><td>Monthly</td></tr><tr><td>Clean dust from the condenser</td><td>Frequently, to prevent overheating</td></tr><tr><td>Replace the water filter</td><td>Per manual</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Vevor recommends purified water for clean ice, and says dust on the condenser can cause poor heat dissipation and damage the compressor. For more on commercial-style care see <a href="/how-to-clean-commercial-ice-maker/">how to clean a commercial ice maker</a>.</p>`],
      ['Who should buy it', `<ul><li>Home bars and garages that need lots of ice for events.</li><li>Seafood stands, farmers market sellers and food trucks that want cheap capacity.</li><li>Small offices.</li></ul><p>Skip it if you need NSF-listed equipment, a local service network, or heavy daily use all day. For a hotel or busy bar, a commercial brand costs more but is built for it.</p>`],
      ['Questions to ask first', `<ul><li>What is the warranty length and who services it?</li><li>Is the unit NSF listed, or only ETL?</li><li>Does it need a drain pump in your location?</li><li>What is the exact model you are buying, and is it the one on the spec sheet?</li></ul>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['Is the Vevor ice maker part of the recall?', 'No. The CPSC recall of December 11, 2025 covers Vevor ice crushers, models BY-300 and BY-300FS. It does not list any Vevor ice makers.'],
      ['How much ice does a Vevor commercial ice maker make?', 'It depends on the model. The 130 lb listing rates up to 130 pounds in 24 hours with a 30 lb bin, and Vevor sells models from about 70 to 200 pounds a day.'],
      ['Does a Vevor ice maker need a drain?', 'The 130 lb model uses an inlet hose and a drain hose, so plan for both.'],
      ['Can a Vevor ice maker keep ice frozen?', 'Vevor says the foam insulation on the 130 lb model keeps ice frozen for 6 to 8 hours with the power off, so it is not a freezer.'],
      ['Is a Vevor ice maker good for a restaurant?', 'It can work for light use, but check NSF listing, local service and warranty first. Commercial brands are built for heavy daily use.'],
      ['What should I do if I own a recalled Vevor ice crusher?', 'Stop using it right away and check the model number on the label on top of the unit against BY-300 and BY-300FS. The CPSC notice explains how to get a full refund from Sanven, the seller. The recall covers units sold from July 2021 through July 2025.'],
      ['How do I check whether any ice maker is recalled?', 'Search the model name on the CPSC recall site before you buy, and again after you buy in case a notice appears later. Register the product with the maker when the option exists.'],
      ['Is a Vevor ice maker good for a home bar?', 'It can be, if you can run a water line and drain. Output and bin size are generous for the price, and the timer and thickness controls are handy. Weigh the thinner support against the savings.'],
    ],
    refs: [['CPSC, Sanven Technology recalls Vevor ice crushers due to fire hazard (December 11, 2025)', 'https://cpsc.gov/Recalls/2026/Sanven-Technology-Recalls-Vevor-Ice-Crushers-Due-to-Fire-Hazard'], ['Recall summary, number 26-147', 'https://recalls.justia.com/household-and-office/appliances/26-147'], ['VEVOR, 130 lb commercial ice maker listing', 'https://www.vevor.com/freestanding-ice-machine-c_40409/vevor-commercial-ice-maker-machine-130-lbs-24h-with-30-lbs-storage-capacity-built-in-freestanding-under-counter-stainless-steel-ice-maker-with-led-display-self-cleaning-for-home-bar-restaurant-p_010274943904'], ['VEVOR, ice machine setup and care guide', 'https://www.vevor.com/diy-ideas/vevor-ice-machine-manual-pdf/'], ['Whynter, UIM-502SS built-in ice maker', 'https://www.whynter.com/product/uim-502ss/'], ['Hoshizaki America, AM-50BAK specifications', 'https://www.hoshizakiamerica.com/product/am-50bak/']],
    next: ['best-undercounter-commercial-ice-maker', 'best-commercial-ice-maker', 'best-freestanding-ice-maker', 'best-ice-maker-for-home-bar', 'how-to-clean-commercial-ice-maker'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'gevi-nugget-ice-maker-review', short: 'Gevi nugget review', blurb: 'GEVI\'s nugget ice makers compared: V2.0, Adella and KNOVA.', review: { name: 'GEVI Household V2.0 Countertop Nugget Ice Maker', brand: 'GEVI', score: 7.8 },
    tab: 'Gevi Nugget Ice Maker Review (2026): V2.0, Adella, KNOVA',
    summary: 'Gevi nugget ice maker review: the V2.0, Adella and KNOVA compared on output, basket size, water line, noise and cleaning, with how they stack up against the GE Opal.',
    heading: 'Gevi nugget ice maker review',
    lede: 'GEVI makes a small family of nugget ice makers, from the water-line-ready V2.0 to the compact KNOVA. They are often discounted, so the right model depends on the counter you have.',
    quick: 'The GEVI Household V2.0 (IMNA0) is the best Gevi for most buyers: a 4.8 lb basket, a 30 lb a day rating, the option to connect a water line, and a two-layer insulated bin. The Adella suits you if you want a side tank and a window, and the KNOVA if you need a small body. All three cost less than a GE Opal when on sale. Our rating for the V2.0: 7.8 out of 10.',
    list: ['GEVI Household V2.0 (IMNA0)', 'GEVI Adella (IMNC0)', 'GEVI KNOVA (IMND0)', 'GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra'],
    parts: [
      ['The verdict', pick('gevi20', 'Our rating: 7.8 out of 10', 'A well-rounded nugget maker with a bigger basket than most, an optional water line and good insulation. It is heavy and not tiny, but it fits under most standard cabinets.')],
      ['The three Gevi models side by side', `<table><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>V2.0 (IMNA0)</th><th>Adella (IMNC0)</th><th>KNOVA (IMND0)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Daily output</td><td>30 lb</td><td>30 lb</td><td>37 lb</td></tr><tr><td>Ice basket</td><td>4.8 lb</td><td>2.5 lb</td><td>2.5 lb</td></tr><tr><td>Reservoir</td><td>2.65 to 2.8 L</td><td>3.0 to 3.78 L</td><td>1.8 L</td></tr><tr><td>First batch</td><td>15 minutes</td><td>20 minutes</td><td>10 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Full basket</td><td>about 4 hours</td><td>about 3 hours</td><td>about 3 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Insulation</td><td>2-layer</td><td>1-layer</td><td>1-layer</td></tr><tr><td>Water refill</td><td>Auto (water line) or manual</td><td>Manual</td><td>Manual</td></tr><tr><td>Size (D x W x H)</td><td>17.3 x 9.7 x 16.7 in</td><td>10.7 x 15.8 x 16.5 in</td><td>12.3 x 9.6 x 15.15 in</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These figures come from Gevi's comparison chart. A fourth model, the IMNB0, matches the V2.0 and adds a viewing window.</p>`],
      ['Gevi V2.0 details', `<ul><li><b>Power.</b> 120 V, 170 W, 3.0 A.</li><li><b>Weight.</b> 37.6 lb net, so it is not a machine to move often.</li><li><b>Water.</b> A 2.65 L reservoir in the support specs, or connect a 1/4 inch water line for automatic refill. Gevi says you can use a faucet adapter if needed.</li><li><b>Cleaning.</b> Self-cleaning, with a recommended clean every two weeks using a cleaning solution, then 3 to 4 rinse cycles with clean water.</li><li><b>Defrost mode.</b> Hold the Clean button for 3 seconds to run a defrost cycle that takes about 30 minutes. Gevi's FAQ points to it if the machine gets loud.</li><li><b>Warranty.</b> 1-year standard warranty, with optional extended coverage up to 3 more years.</li></ul><p>Gevi lists about 120 to 170 watts for its ice makers, much less than a refrigerator, so running costs are small.</p>`],
      ['Gevi Adella', pick('geviadella', 'Best for a side tank and a window', 'The Adella adds a side tank of about 3 to 3.8 L (Gevi lists both figures) and a viewing window so you can watch water level and ice. The tank cuts refills, but the basket holds only 2.5 pounds and the body is wide.')],
      ['Gevi KNOVA', pick('geviknova', 'Best compact Gevi', 'Rated at 37 pounds a day with a 10 minute first batch, in a 9.6 inch wide body. The small 1.8 L reservoir and single-layer insulation are the trade-offs.')],
      ['Gevi vs GE Opal and rivals', `<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Rated output</th><th>Basket or bin</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>${nameLink('gevi20')}</td><td>30 lb a day</td><td>4.8 lb</td><td>Water line option, 2-layer insulation</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('opalultra')}</td><td>38 lb a day</td><td>3 lb</td><td>GE brand, side tank, app, scale-inhibiting filter</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('euhomynugget')}</td><td>34 lb a day</td><td>1.8 lb</td><td>Cheaper, compact</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('ecozy451')}</td><td>44 lb a day</td><td>Not listed</td><td>Smart panel, TDS monitor</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Gevi V2.0 gives you a larger basket than the Opal and a water line option. The Opal gives you more output, GE's service network and an app. See <a href="/ge-profile-opal-2-review/">our GE Opal review</a>.</p>`],
      ['Noise, placement and fit', `<ul><li>Place it on a flat, dry surface away from heat and direct sun, and close to a sink if you plan to drain it.</li><li>The V2.0 is about 16.7 inches tall, which fits under most cabinets that sit 17.5 to 18 inches above the counter.</li><li>It is a compressor machine, so it hums and rattles ice into the basket. Gevi calls it quieter than a dishwasher; judge by owner feedback and your own room.</li><li>It is normal for water to flow from the top for the first 5 minutes of a cycle. A hidden tank above the freezing parts fills from the reservoir.</li></ul>`],
      ['Does it keep ice frozen', `<p>Gevi says its countertop nugget makers store ice in an insulated bin for easy access, and it adds that the machine is not a freezer. The V2.0 has two insulating layers and the Adella and KNOVA have one. Expect ice to melt slowly over hours, with the water returning to the reservoir to be remade. If you want to keep ice for days, move it to the freezer. See <a href="/countertop-ice-maker-that-keeps-ice-frozen/">why countertop ice makers do not keep ice frozen</a>.</p>`],
      ['Cleaning and care', `<ol><li>Unplug the machine and empty the water.</li><li>Run the self-cleaning cycle with a cleaning solution made for ice makers.</li><li>Run 3 to 4 cycles of clean water to rinse out the cleaner.</li><li>Wipe the interior and the basket.</li><li>Use filtered or purified water to cut down on scale.</li></ol><p>For products see <a href="/best-ice-maker-cleaning-solution/">best ice maker cleaners</a>.</p>`],
      ['Who should buy it', `<ul><li>Buyers who want a roomy basket and an automatic water line option for a kitchen with a sink nearby.</li><li>Nugget fans who want to spend less than an Opal.</li></ul><p>Skip it if you need an app-controlled machine from a large service network, or if lifting 37 pounds is a problem.</p>`],
      ['Which Gevi to pick', `<table><thead><tr><th>If you want</th><th>Pick</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The biggest basket and a water line option</td><td>V2.0 (IMNA0)</td></tr><tr><td>The same machine with a viewing window</td><td>IMNB0</td></tr><tr><td>A side tank to cut refills</td><td>Adella (IMNC0)</td></tr><tr><td>The smallest body and fastest first batch</td><td>KNOVA (IMND0)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If your sink is close to the counter, the V2.0's water line option removes the chore of refilling. If you have no sink nearby, a side tank on the Adella is the next best thing.</p>`],
      ['Setup in short', `<ol><li>Set the machine on a flat surface, away from heat and direct sun.</li><li>Rinse the basket and reservoir, then run a clean cycle before the first batch.</li><li>Fill the reservoir with filtered or purified water, or connect a 1/4 inch line to the inlet on the V2.0.</li><li>Press start. The first nuggets arrive in about 10 to 20 minutes depending on the model.</li><li>Let the basket fill. Gevi lists about 3 to 4 hours for a full basket.</li></ol>`],
      ['Troubleshooting', `<table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>What Gevi suggests or what to check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Water flows from the ice outlet</td><td>Normal for about the first 5 minutes of a cycle</td></tr><tr><td>Machine is loud</td><td>Run defrost mode: hold the Clean button for 3 seconds, about 30 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Small or slow batches</td><td>Check room heat and water temperature, and clean the unit</td></tr><tr><td>Ice tastes off</td><td>Clean, rinse 3 to 4 times, and use filtered water</td></tr><tr><td>Mold or slime</td><td>Clean more often than every two weeks, and dry the interior when not in use</td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['Why nugget ice', `<p>Nugget ice is made from compressed flakes, so it is soft, airy and easy to chew. It also absorbs the flavor of drinks, which is why it is popular for sodas, iced coffee and smoothies. If you are new to the idea, read <a href="/what-is-nugget-ice/">what nugget ice is</a> before you buy, because it melts faster in a large glass than a big cube.</p>`],
      ['Verdict', `<p>The Gevi V2.0 is a good-value nugget maker with a bigger basket and water line option than most. The Adella and KNOVA fill niches. Our rating is 7.8 out of 10.</p>${buy('gevi20', 'Check Gevi V2.0 price on Amazon')}`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['How much ice does the Gevi nugget ice maker make?', 'The V2.0 and Adella are rated at 30 pounds a day and the KNOVA at 37 pounds a day, per Gevi\'s comparison chart.'],
      ['Does the Gevi V2.0 connect to a water line?', 'Yes. It can refill automatically from a 1/4 inch water line, or you can fill the reservoir by hand.'],
      ['How often should I clean a Gevi ice maker?', 'Gevi recommends every two weeks, using a cleaning solution, then rinsing with 3 to 4 cycles of clean water.'],
      ['Is Gevi as good as the Opal?', 'The Gevi makes similar nugget ice for less, with a bigger basket on the V2.0. The Opal has a higher rating, an app and a larger service network.'],
      ['What warranty does Gevi offer?', 'A 1-year standard warranty, with optional extra coverage up to 3 more years.'],
      ['Does Gevi make a clear nugget ice maker?', 'The Adella is sold as a crystal clear nugget ice maker with a viewing window and a side water tank. Gevi says the window lets you watch water level and ice formation.'],
      ['Where should I put a Gevi ice maker?', 'On a flat, dry surface away from heat and direct sunlight, ideally near a sink so you can drain and clean it.'],
    ],
    refs: [['GEVI, ice maker collection and comparison chart', 'https://gevi.com/collections/ice-makers'], ['GEVI, IMNA0 support specs and FAQ', 'https://gevi.com/pages/support-for-imna0'], ['GEVI, Household V2.0 nugget ice maker', 'https://gevi.com/products/countertop-nugget-ice-maker-and-machine-for-home'], ['GEVI, Adella nugget ice maker', 'https://gevi.com/products/gevi-adella-ice-maker'], ['GE Appliances, Opal nugget ice makers', 'https://www.geappliances.com/ge/icemakers/opal-nugget-icemaker.htm']],
    next: ['best-nugget-ice-maker', 'ge-profile-opal-2-review', 'best-cheap-nugget-ice-maker', 'ecozy-nugget-ice-maker-review', 'euhomy-nugget-ice-maker-review'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'costway-nugget-ice-maker-review', short: 'Costway nugget review', blurb: 'Costway\'s countertop nugget ice makers, including the self-dispensing models.', review: { name: 'Costway Nugget Ice Maker Countertop (44 lb)', brand: 'Costway', score: 7.6 },
    tab: 'Costway Nugget Countertop Ice Maker Review (2026): 44, 56, 80 lb',
    summary: 'Costway nugget countertop ice maker review: the 44 lb, 56 lb and 80 lb self-dispensing models, specs, noise, cleaning and warranty, and how they compare.',
    heading: 'Costway nugget countertop ice maker review',
    lede: 'Costway sells a long list of nugget ice makers, including self-dispensing models. This review covers the main ones, what the spec sheets say, and who should buy.',
    quick: 'Costway\'s 44 lb nugget ice maker is a good-value, fast nugget machine with self-cleaning and a 365-day warranty. The 56 lb self-dispensing model adds a lever so you do not scoop, at the cost of more noise. Both cost far less than an Opal. Skip them if you want quiet running or a long track record. Our rating for the 44 lb model: 7.6 out of 10.',
    list: ['Costway Nugget Ice Maker Countertop (44 lb)', 'Costway Self-Dispensing Nugget Ice Maker (56 lb)', 'GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra', 'GEVI Household V2.0', 'Euhomy Nugget Ice Maker (34 lb)'],
    parts: [
      ['The verdict', pick('costway44', 'Our rating: 7.6 out of 10', 'A fast, compact, well-priced nugget maker with a self-cleaning cycle and a removable drip tray. It is the best Costway for most people. Expect more noise than an Opal and plan on frequent refills.')],
      ['Costway nugget lineup', `<table><thead><tr><th>Model (from the maker's range)</th><th>Rated output</th><th>What it adds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nugget countertop with removable drip tray</td><td>44 lb a day</td><td>The core model: 2.7 lb basket, 0.47 gal tank, 6 to 15 minute first batch</td></tr><tr><td>Nugget with audible ice-full alarm</td><td>Check the listing</td><td>Lower-priced model with a full-basket alarm</td></tr><tr><td>Self-dispensing nugget</td><td>56 lb a day</td><td>Dispenser lever, 3.3 lb bin, 0.63 gal tank</td></tr><tr><td>Self-dispensing nugget, large</td><td>80 lb a day</td><td>Bigger output for families and offices</td></tr><tr><td>Commercial nugget</td><td>90 lb a day</td><td>Self-cleaning, aimed at small businesses</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Costway also sells crescent, clear cube, sphere and commercial cube ice makers. Check the exact model name and number against the figures you want.</p>`],
      ['What the 44 lb model offers', `<ul><li><b>Output.</b> Costway rates it up to 44 lb in 24 hours.</li><li><b>Speed.</b> The first batch takes 6 to 15 minutes, and the machine keeps making nuggets continuously until the basket is full.</li><li><b>Basket and tank.</b> 2.7 lb basket and a 0.47 gallon water tank.</li><li><b>Cleaning.</b> A self-cleaning function and a removable drip tray.</li><li><b>Warranty.</b> Costway lists a 365-day warranty and 30-day returns.</li></ul><p>Owner feedback shown on the product page highlights fast, consistent nugget ice, easy use, and quiet operation, and frequent concerns include quick melting due to lack of insulation and the need for frequent water refills. It suits personal or small-family use, not large parties.</p>`],
      ['Self-dispensing model', pick('costwayself', 'Best for no-scoop ice', 'The self-dispensing model has a lever, so you press a glass against it and nuggets drop out. It lists a 3.3 lb bin, 0.63 gal tank, a first cycle of about 15 minutes and 56 lb a day, in a body about 11 inches wide and 16 inches tall.')],
      ['What owners say about the dispenser', `<p>Reviews on the product page praise fast ice production, easy controls, self-cleaning, the convenient water tank and the dispenser lever. Common concerns are loud operation and sometimes slow dispensing. One owner notes it takes longer to make ice than listed and does not quite reach the rated output, which is common for countertop machines. Treat the rated output as a ceiling.</p>`],
      ['Costway vs the competition', `<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Rated output</th><th>Basket or bin</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>${nameLink('costway44')}</td><td>44 lb a day</td><td>2.7 lb</td><td>365-day warranty, fast first batch</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('costwayself')}</td><td>56 lb a day</td><td>3.3 lb</td><td>Dispenser lever</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('opalultra')}</td><td>38 lb a day</td><td>3 lb</td><td>GE brand, app, side tank</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('gevi20')}</td><td>30 lb a day</td><td>4.8 lb</td><td>Water line option</td></tr><tr><td>${nameLink('euhomynugget')}</td><td>34 lb a day</td><td>1.8 lb</td><td>Low price</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Costway rates its machines at higher output than the Opal at a lower price. The Opal wins on service, quiet running and refinement. See <a href="/ge-profile-opal-2-review/">our Opal review</a>.</p>`],
      ['Does it keep ice frozen', `<p>No. Like any countertop nugget machine, ice sits in a basket that is not a freezer, so it slowly melts and the water goes back to the tank to be remade. The product page notes quick melting due to limited insulation. If you want to keep ice, store it in a freezer bag. See <a href="/countertop-ice-maker-that-keeps-ice-frozen/">why countertop ice makers do not keep ice frozen</a>.</p>`],
      ['Setup, cleaning and care', `<ol><li>Place it on a level counter near an outlet, with room around it for airflow.</li><li>Rinse the tank and basket, then run the self-cleaning cycle before the first batch.</li><li>Fill the tank with filtered or purified water. Hard water builds scale fast in nugget machines.</li><li>Press start and wait for the first batch, then use the machine as you need ice.</li><li>On the dispenser, press the lever with a glass, and empty the drip tray when it fills.</li><li>Clean every few weeks, more often if you use it daily or have hard water.</li></ol><p>See <a href="/how-to-clean-portable-ice-maker/">how to clean a portable ice maker</a> for general steps and <a href="/best-ice-maker-cleaning-solution/">best ice maker cleaners</a> for products. Follow the Costway manual where it differs.</p>`],
      ['Who should buy a Costway', `<ul><li>Nugget fans who want lots of rated output for the money.</li><li>Households that want a no-scoop dispenser.</li><li>Buyers who like a one-year warranty and a 30-day return window.</li></ul><p>Skip it if noise matters, if you want an app and a big service network, or if you want to store ice for days.</p>`],
      ['How often you will refill the tank', `<p>Nugget machines make ice from water, so the tank empties as ice is made, and it refills partly from melt water that drains back. You can estimate the refill rhythm from the specs. One pound of water makes one pound of ice, and a liter of water weighs about 2.2 pounds.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Tank</th><th>Tank in pounds of water</th><th>Rated output per hour</th><th>Hours of nonstop making per tank</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>44 lb nugget</td><td>0.47 gal (about 1.8 L)</td><td>about 3.9 lb</td><td>44 / 24 = about 1.8 lb</td><td>about 2.1 hours</td></tr><tr><td>56 lb self-dispensing</td><td>0.63 gal (about 2.4 L)</td><td>about 5.3 lb</td><td>56 / 24 = about 2.3 lb</td><td>about 2.3 hours</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Real life is gentler because the machine stops when the basket is full and recycles melt water. Still, plan on refilling the tank a few times a day if you run it hard. That is the biggest day-to-day cost of the Costway design, and it is why side tanks and water line connections exist on other brands. See the <a href="/ge-profile-opal-2-review/">GE Opal</a> and <a href="/gevi-nugget-ice-maker-review/">Gevi V2.0</a> if refills bother you.</p>`],
      ['How fast the basket fills', `<p>A basket stops production when it is full, so the basket size tells you how much ice is ready at once. Using the rated output as a ceiling:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Basket</th><th>Time to fill at rated speed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>44 lb nugget</td><td>2.7 lb</td><td>about 1.5 hours (2.7 / 1.8 per hour)</td></tr><tr><td>56 lb self-dispensing</td><td>3.3 lb</td><td>about 1.4 hours (3.3 / 2.3 per hour)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both are comfortable for daily drinks and a small family. For a party, start well ahead and bag the ice. At about 1.5 pounds per guest, 10 guests need roughly 15 pounds, which is about 8 hours of nonstop production from the 44 lb model.</p>`],
      ['Running cost', `<p>The self-dispensing model lists 115 watts. At an example rate of 15 cents per kilowatt hour, running it for 6 hours a day uses 0.69 kWh, about 10 cents a day. Running it nonstop for 24 hours uses about 2.8 kWh, or roughly 41 cents. Real use falls between those because the machine stops when the basket is full. Electricity is a small cost compared with filtered water and cleaning supplies.</p>`],
      ['Water quality and scale', `<p>Nugget machines run water over a cold evaporator, so minerals collect there. Other brands set a limit: ecozy's TDS monitor warns above 200 ppm and the maker recommends purified or bottled water. Costway does not publish a TDS limit on the pages we read, but the same logic applies. Use filtered or purified water if your tap water is hard, descale on schedule, and rinse well afterward. White film on the evaporator, a gritty feel in the ice, or slower output are signs of scale.</p>`],
      ['Troubleshooting by symptom', `<table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>What to try</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Slow or small batches</td><td>Warm room, warm water or scale</td><td>Use cold water, move the machine away from heat, descale</td></tr><tr><td>Ice melts quickly</td><td>Limited basket insulation</td><td>Scoop ice into a freezer bag, and keep the lid closed</td></tr><tr><td>Loud while making ice</td><td>Normal compressor and auger noise, or an unlevel counter</td><td>Level the machine, and place it on a stable surface</td></tr><tr><td>Water in the drip tray</td><td>Melt water or overfill</td><td>Empty the tray, check the tank line</td></tr><tr><td>Ice-full alarm keeps sounding</td><td>Basket is full</td><td>Remove ice, or turn off the alarm if the model allows</td></tr><tr><td>Off taste</td><td>Dirty basket or tank</td><td>Run the self-clean cycle and rinse</td></tr><tr><td>No ice, pump running</td><td>Low water or an air lock</td><td>Refill the tank and reseat it</td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['The rest of Costway\'s ice makers', `<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>From the maker's range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Crescent</td><td>Countertop, 26 lb a day, 16 crescent cubes per batch</td></tr><tr><td>Clear ice cube</td><td>Countertop, 1.6 inch large square cubes</td></tr><tr><td>Sphere</td><td>2 inch ice ball makers: 4 balls in 38 minutes, or 8 hour timer with self-cleaning</td></tr><tr><td>Commercial nugget</td><td>90 lb a day with self-cleaning</td></tr><tr><td>Commercial cube</td><td>110 lb a day countertop, and larger freestanding models up to 450 lb a day</td></tr><tr><td>Undercounter</td><td>90 lb a day built-in or freestanding</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For sphere and clear models see <a href="/best-clear-ice-maker/">clear ice makers</a>. Commercial claims on any consumer brand need a check: ask whether the unit is NSF listed before you put it in a restaurant, and check the warranty terms for commercial use.</p>`],
      ['Costway nugget vs Sonic-style ice', `<p>Nugget ice is the soft, chewable ice made by compressing flakes into small pieces. It is the same style that drive-in restaurants made popular. A home nugget maker gets close, but results depend on water, machine and how long the ice sits. If you want the details, see <a href="/sonic-ice-at-home/">how to get Sonic ice at home</a> and <a href="/what-is-nugget-ice/">what nugget ice is</a>.</p>`],
      ['Buying checklist', `<ol><li>Pick the output you need, and treat it as a ceiling.</li><li>Check the basket size, since it controls how much ice is ready.</li><li>Check the tank size and how you will refill it.</li><li>Decide whether you want a dispenser lever or a scoop.</li><li>Confirm height under your cabinets.</li><li>Read the warranty and return terms.</li><li>Plan on filtered water and regular cleaning.</li></ol>`],
      ['Costway reliability and support', `<p>Costway lists a 365-day warranty and a 30-day return window on its nugget pages, and says it ships most in-stock orders within one business day. It is a large online appliance seller rather than a long-established ice machine brand, so parts and service are different from GE. Register your purchase, keep your receipt, and test the machine fully in the first 30 days so you can use the return window if it is not what you wanted.</p>`],
      ['Which Costway nugget size to buy', `<table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Best fit</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One or two drinkers, small kitchen</td><td>44 lb nugget</td><td>Enough output, small tank refills are manageable</td></tr><tr><td>Family of four who all use ice</td><td>56 lb self-dispensing</td><td>Bigger bin and tank, no scooping</td></tr><tr><td>Office break room</td><td>80 lb self-dispensing</td><td>Highest rated output in the consumer line</td></tr><tr><td>Small cafe or food stand</td><td>Commercial nugget (90 lb)</td><td>Check NSF listing and warranty for business use first</td></tr><tr><td>Tight budget</td><td>Lower-priced nugget with alarm</td><td>Fewer features, same style of ice</td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['Where to put it', `<ul><li>Choose a level counter near an outlet. Nugget machines vibrate slightly, so avoid a wobbly surface.</li><li>Keep it out of direct sun and away from the oven or toaster. Heat lowers output.</li><li>Leave space at the sides and back for airflow. A tight cabinet nook raises temperatures.</li><li>Measure height, especially for the 16 inch self-dispensing model, and allow room to lift the lid or reach the tank.</li><li>Keep a towel or tray where the dispenser drips.</li></ul>`],
      ['Moving, storing and going away', `<p>Before a vacation or a move, unplug the machine, drain the tank, empty the basket, and leave the lid open so the inside dries. Standing water in a closed machine can grow slime. Let the machine sit upright for a few hours after moving it so the compressor oil settles, then run a clean cycle before using it again. Store it upright in its box if you can.</p>`],
      ['Costway nugget vs other budget brands', `<p>Budget nugget makers from Costway, Euhomy, Gevi, Kismile, Cowsar and others are built on similar designs. The differences that matter are output rating, basket and tank size, dispenser or no dispenser, water line option, warranty and noise. Costway stands out for high rated output and a dispenser option. Gevi stands out for a bigger basket and water line option. Euhomy and Kismile stand out for price and size. Read the reviews of <a href="/euhomy-nugget-ice-maker-review/">Euhomy</a>, <a href="/kismile-nugget-ice-maker-review/">Kismile</a> and <a href="/cowsar-nugget-ice-maker-review/">Cowsar</a> to compare, and see <a href="/best-cheap-nugget-ice-maker/">cheap nugget ice makers</a> for a shortlist.</p>`],
      ['What the spec sheet does not tell you', `<p>Spec sheets list rated output, tank and basket, but not how the machine behaves day to day. Ask these questions of owner reviews before you buy: how loud is the compressor and the ice drop, how long does it really take to fill the basket, how often does the tank need refilling, how well does the dispenser work with tall glasses, and how does the machine handle hard water after a few months? Owner feedback on the dispenser model points to noise and slow dispensing as the main complaints, so decide how much those matter in your home before you pay for the lever.</p>`],
      ['Verdict', `<p>Costway is a solid value in nugget ice. The 44 lb model is the safe pick and the self-dispensing 56 lb is the convenience pick. Our rating for the 44 lb is 7.6 out of 10.</p>${buy('costway44', 'Check Costway nugget price on Amazon')}`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['How much ice does the Costway nugget ice maker make?', 'The standard model is rated up to 44 pounds in 24 hours, and the self-dispensing models are listed at 56 and 80 pounds a day.'],
      ['Does the Costway nugget ice maker dispense ice?', 'The self-dispensing models do. You press a glass against a lever and ice drops out.'],
      ['Is Costway nugget ice the same as Sonic ice?', 'Both are soft, chewable nugget ice. See how to get that ice at home in our guide.'],
      ['What warranty does Costway offer?', 'The product pages list a 365-day warranty and 30-day returns.'],
      ['Is the Costway ice maker loud?', 'Owner feedback on the dispenser models mentions noise while making ice. It is quieter once the basket is full.'],
      ['How long does a Costway ice maker take to make ice?', 'The 44 lb nugget model lists a first batch in 6 to 15 minutes, and the self-dispensing 56 lb model lists about 15 minutes. After that it keeps making ice until the basket is full.'],
      ['Can a Costway nugget ice maker connect to a water line?', 'The models we read use a manual-fill water tank. If you want a water line, look at the GEVI V2.0, which offers an automatic refill option.'],
      ['Does the Costway ice maker need to be cleaned?', 'Yes. It has a self-cleaning function, and Costway recommends cleaning every few weeks, more often with heavy use or hard water.'],
      ['Is a Costway ice maker worth it compared with an Opal?', 'If you want lots of rated output and a dispenser for a lower price, yes. If you want quiet running, an app and a service network, the Opal is worth the premium.'],
      ['How big is the Costway self-dispensing ice maker?', 'The listing shows about 11 inches wide and 16 inches tall, and 115 watts.'],
    ],
    refs: [['Costway, nugget ice maker countertop', 'https://www.costway.com/nugget-ice-maker-countertop-with-soft-chewable-pellet.html'], ['Costway, self-dispensing nugget ice maker', 'https://www.costway.com/self-dispensing-nugget-ice-maker-countertop-with-soft-chewable-pellet-ice-cubes.html'], ['Costway, ice makers', 'https://www.costway.com/appliance/ice-makers.html'], ['GEVI, ice maker comparison chart', 'https://gevi.com/collections/ice-makers'], ['GE Appliances, Opal nugget ice makers', 'https://www.geappliances.com/ge/icemakers/opal-nugget-icemaker.htm']],
    next: ['best-nugget-ice-maker', 'best-cheap-nugget-ice-maker', 'ge-profile-opal-2-review', 'gevi-nugget-ice-maker-review', 'sonic-ice-at-home'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'ge-opal-vs-opal-2', short: 'GE Opal vs Opal 2.0', blurb: 'Original Opal vs Opal 2.0, Ultra, XL and Mini: what changed and which to buy.',
    tab: 'GE Opal vs Opal 2.0: Differences and Which to Buy (2026)',
    summary: 'GE Opal vs Opal 2.0: what changed from the original Opal, how the 2.0, 2.0 Ultra, 2.0 XL and Mini compare on output, size, filter and app, and which one to buy.',
    heading: 'GE Opal vs Opal 2.0',
    lede: 'GE now sells several Opals, and the names are confusing. Here is how the original Opal compares with the Opal 2.0 family, and which version to buy.',
    quick: 'Buy an Opal 2.0 family model, not the original Opal. The 2.0 and 2.0 Ultra are rated up to 38 pounds of nugget ice a day on current GE listings (older listings say 24), with WiFi instead of the original\'s Bluetooth. Choose the Ultra for the included scale-inhibiting filter, the 2.0 XL for the 1-gallon side tank, and the Mini only if width matters most.',
    list: ['GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra', 'GE Profile Opal 2.0 XL', 'GE Profile Opal 2.0', 'GE Profile Opal Mini', 'GE Profile Opal (original)'],
    parts: [
      ['The Opal family at a glance', `<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Daily output</th><th>Per hour</th><th>Width x depth</th><th>Filter</th><th>Connectivity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Opal (original, "01") with side tank</td><td>Up to 34 lb</td><td>1.3 lb</td><td>14.25 x 15.5 in</td><td>Carbon included</td><td>Bluetooth</td></tr><tr><td>Opal 2.0 XL (2.0 plus 1 gal side tank)</td><td>Up to 38 lb</td><td>1.6 lb</td><td>13.43 x 17.5 in</td><td>Carbon included</td><td>WiFi</td></tr><tr><td>Opal 2.0 Ultra</td><td>Up to 38 lb</td><td>1.6 lb</td><td>10.5 x 17.5 in</td><td>Scale inhibiting, included</td><td>WiFi</td></tr><tr><td>Opal 2.0 Ultra XL (1 gal side tank)</td><td>Up to 38 lb</td><td>1.6 lb</td><td>13.43 x 17.5 in</td><td>Scale inhibiting, included</td><td>WiFi</td></tr><tr><td>Opal Mini</td><td>Up to 36 lb</td><td>1.5 lb</td><td>8.6 x 15.6 in</td><td>Scale inhibiting compatible</td><td>WiFi</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These figures come from GE's current Opal lineup page. Older 2.0 listings and many reviews quote 24 pounds a day for the 2.0, which GE has since raised to 38 on current listings. If you see two numbers, the newer GE listing is the one to trust.</p>`],
      ['What changed from the original to 2.0', `<ul><li><b>Faster ice.</b> GE lists 1.6 pounds an hour for the 2.0 and Ultra, against 1.3 for the original.</li><li><b>WiFi and voice.</b> The 2.0 uses WiFi with the SmartHQ app and works with Amazon and Google voice devices to schedule ice, show maintenance alerts, update software and reorder accessories. The original uses Bluetooth.</li><li><b>Controls and bin.</b> The 2.0 has touch controls and a redesigned bin that stops melt water dripping on the counter when you pull the bin.</li><li><b>Quieter operation.</b> Owner feedback says the 2.0 is quieter than the original.</li><li><b>Filters.</b> The Ultra includes a scale-inhibiting filter. The 2.0 XL comes with a carbon filter.</li><li><b>Brewpal compatibility.</b> GE's Brewpal coffee maker pairs with full-size Opal 2.0 and Ultra models, but not the original.</li></ul>`],
      ['Opal 2.0 vs 2.0 Ultra', pick('opalultra', 'Best Opal to buy', 'The Ultra is the current top model: the same 38 lb a day and 1.6 lb per hour as the 2.0, plus an included scale-inhibiting filter. With the side tank it measures 16.5 x 13.43 x 17.5 inches (H x W x D) and holds a 3 lb bin.')],
      ['Opal 2.0 XL', pick('opalxl', 'Best for fewer refills', 'An Opal 2.0 with a 1 gallon XL side tank. It has the same 38 lb a day and 3 lb bin as the Ultra, but comes with a carbon filter rather than the scale-inhibiting one. Buy it when the price is clearly lower than the Ultra.')],
      ['Opal Mini', pick('opalmini', 'Best narrow Opal', 'The Mini is 8.6 inches wide and 15.6 inches deep, and GE lists up to 36 pounds a day at 1.5 pounds an hour, with a first batch in about 5 minutes. It has a smaller bin than the full-size Opal. Choose it for tight counters.')],
      ['What stays the same', `<ul><li>Nugget ice made in batches: water is filtered and recirculated to make fresh ice.</li><li>No water line needed. You fill a reservoir or a side tank.</li><li>The bin is insulated, not a freezer. Ice slowly melts and the water returns to the reservoir.</li><li>The first ice drops in about 10 minutes after startup on the 2.0.</li><li>Cleaning and descaling are required. GE suggests descaling every 2 to 4 months, and more often in hard water. See <a href="/how-to-descale-ge-opal/">how to descale a GE Opal</a> and <a href="/how-to-clean-ge-opal-ice-maker/">how to clean a GE Opal</a>.</li></ul>`],
      ['Which should you buy', `<table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Buy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>You want the best Opal and have hard water</td><td>Opal 2.0 Ultra (included scale-inhibiting filter)</td></tr><tr><td>You hate refilling</td><td>Opal 2.0 XL or Ultra XL (1 gallon side tank)</td></tr><tr><td>Counter space is tight</td><td>Opal Mini</td></tr><tr><td>You find an original Opal very cheap</td><td>Possible, but you lose WiFi, speed and Brewpal pairing</td></tr><tr><td>You want nugget ice for far less money</td><td>A budget nugget maker; see <a href="/best-cheap-nugget-ice-maker/">cheap nugget ice makers</a></td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['Buying an original Opal used', `<p>Original Opals appear secondhand at big discounts. They still make good nugget ice, but check the unit before you pay: run a full clean cycle, check for scale on the evaporator, confirm the pump runs, and ask when it was last descaled. Expect Bluetooth control only, a slower rate, and parts that are older. If the price gap to a new 2.0 is small, buy new.</p>`],
      ['Keeping any Opal running', `<p>Use filtered or distilled water to reduce scale, clean on a schedule, and descale when output drops. If your Opal stops making ice, see <a href="/ge-opal-not-making-ice/">Opal not making ice</a>. For filters, see <a href="/ge-opal-water-filter/">the GE Opal water filter guide</a>. For a full review, read the <a href="/ge-profile-opal-2-review/">GE Profile Opal 2.0 review</a> and the <a href="/ge-opal-2-xl-review/">Opal 2.0 XL review</a>.</p>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['What is the difference between the Opal and Opal 2.0?', 'The 2.0 is faster (1.6 lb an hour vs 1.3 on GE\'s listings), uses WiFi instead of Bluetooth, and has a redesigned bin and controls. Current GE listings rate the 2.0 at up to 38 pounds a day.'],
      ['Is the Opal 2.0 worth the extra money over the original?', 'Usually yes. You get more output, better app control and quieter operation. A very cheap used original can still make sense.'],
      ['What is the difference between the Opal 2.0 and the 2.0 XL?', 'The 2.0 XL is the Opal 2.0 plus a 1 gallon XL side tank, so you refill less often.'],
      ['Is the Opal 2.0 Ultra better than the 2.0?', 'It makes the same amount of ice, but includes a scale-inhibiting filter. That helps if your water is hard.'],
      ['How much ice does the Opal 2.0 make?', 'GE lists up to 38 pounds a day and 1.6 pounds an hour on current Opal 2.0 and Ultra listings. Older listings state 24 pounds a day.'],
      ['Does the Opal need a water line?', 'No. It has a built-in reservoir, and some models add a side tank.'],
    ],
    refs: [['GE Appliances, Opal nugget ice makers (lineup, specs, FAQ)', 'https://www.geappliances.com/ge/icemakers/opal-nugget-icemaker.htm'], ['GE Appliances, Opal 2.0 Ultra comparison sheet', 'https://products.geappliances.com/appliance/gea-compare/%26sku%3DXPIO13SWBB'], ['GE Appliances, Opal cleaning and descaling', 'https://products.geappliances.com/appliance/gea-support-search-content?contentId=000060634']],
    next: ['ge-profile-opal-2-review', 'ge-opal-2-xl-review', 'opal-nugget-ice-maker-sale', 'how-to-descale-ge-opal', 'best-nugget-ice-maker', 'ge-opal-water-filter'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'sonic-ice-at-home', short: 'Sonic ice at home', blurb: 'How to get Sonic-style nugget ice at home: bagged ice, countertop makers and DIY tricks.', 
    tab: 'How to Get Sonic Ice at Home: Bags, Makers and Tricks',
    summary: 'Want Sonic ice at home? Buy it bagged where sold, or run a countertop nugget ice maker. How the ice is made, what each route costs in effort, and DIY shortcuts.',
    heading: 'How to get Sonic ice at home',
    lede: 'The soft, chewable ice at Sonic Drive-In is nugget ice, also called pebble ice. You can get it at home three ways: buy a bag, own a nugget ice maker, or settle for a close imitation. Here is how each route works and who it suits.',
    quick: 'The simplest route is a bag of ice from a Sonic drive-in, and Chick-fil-A also sells bags of ice. For ice on demand, a countertop nugget ice maker makes the same style of ice from tap or filtered water. Crushing regular cubes gets you part of the way, but the texture is different.',
    list: ['GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra', 'Gevi Household V2.0', 'Euhomy Nugget Ice Maker', 'ecozy 44 lb Nugget Ice Maker', 'Costway 44 lb Nugget Ice Maker'],
    parts: [
      ['What Sonic ice actually is', `<p>Sonic ice is nugget ice: small, soft, airy pieces that are easy to chew. Sonic has used nugget ice since the mid-1980s, made by a machine designed by Scotsman, and that the process compresses ice flakes into a cylinder. The air pockets in each piece are why the ice soaks up the flavor of a drink and still tastes good once the drink is gone.</p><p>Nugget ice is not a Sonic trademark. Other makers sell the same kind of machine under different names; Ice-O-Matic calls it "Pearl" and Hoshizaki calls it "Cubelet". The ice is compacted flakes that are soft, airy and easy to munch. Our <a href="/what-is-nugget-ice/">guide to nugget ice</a> covers the type in more detail.</p><p>Sonic says it sold almost 78 million pounds of its pebble ice in a 12-month stretch, counted by the 10-pound bag, according to a Sonic category manager. That tells you the bag is a normal, regular product at the chain, not a favor.</p>`],
      ['Route 1: buy a bag of Sonic ice', `<p>Sonic sells 10-pound bags of its nugget ice, and Chick-fil-A sells bags of ice too. That is the lowest-commitment way to try the ice before you spend on a machine.</p><p>Things to know before you go:</p><ul><li><b>Availability varies.</b> Call the location first. Whether a store sells bags, and how much it charges, is the store's call.</li><li><b>Bring a cooler.</b> The bag starts melting the moment it leaves the freezer. Nugget ice has a lot of surface area for its size, so plan on a short trip home.</li><li><b>Refreeze with care.</b> Melted and refrozen nuggets turn into a solid block. Pour off the water and keep the bag in your freezer, then break the pieces apart before you use them.</li><li><b>It is a recurring errand.</b> If you want ice every day, or for a party, the trips add up.</li></ul><p>Bagged ice makes the most sense for an occasional treat, a one-off party or a taste test. If you keep going back, the next route may fit better.</p>`],
      ['Route 2: make nugget ice with a countertop machine', `<p>A countertop nugget ice maker shaves ice flakes inside the unit and presses them into nuggets, so it produces the same style of ice as a restaurant machine at a smaller scale. These machines hold a water tank and a small bin, and they add more water to the reservoir as the bin fills. They differ in rated output per day, bin size, noise and cleaning features.</p><p>Nugget makers use more complex internal parts than basic cube makers, which is why they usually cost more. The models we list below are widely sold and cover a range of sizes. See our <a href="/best-nugget-ice-maker/">best nugget ice maker</a> page for the full comparison.</p>${pick('opalultra', 'Best for a Sonic-style home setup', 'The GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra is rated up to 38 lb per day on current GE listings, with a 3 lb bin and a side tank option. See our <a href="/ge-profile-opal-2-review/">Opal 2.0 review</a> for details.')}${pick('gevi20', 'Larger bin', 'A 4.8 lb bin means more ice before it stops, which helps at parties.')}${pick('euhomynugget', 'Mid-range option', 'A popular countertop pick with a similar size and output class.')}${pick('ecozy451', 'Budget-friendly pick', 'A 44 lb rated model aimed at families.')}${pick('costway44', 'Lower-cost Costway option', 'A countertop model with a 2.7 lb bin.')}<p>Check each listing for the current output rating, since manufacturers revise specs. Our <a href="/gevi-nugget-ice-maker-review/">Gevi review</a>, <a href="/costway-nugget-ice-maker-review/">Costway review</a> and <a href="/ecozy-nugget-ice-maker-review/">ecozy review</a> cover individual models.</p>`],
      ['Bag versus machine: how to decide', `<table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Bag from a drive-in</th><th>Countertop nugget maker</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Upfront cost</td><td>None beyond the bag</td><td>Cost of the machine</td></tr><tr><td>Ice on demand</td><td>No, only when you shop</td><td>Yes, once the bin has filled</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>Needs freezer space</td><td>Needs counter space and a water supply</td></tr><tr><td>Upkeep</td><td>None</td><td>Regular cleaning and descaling</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Occasional use, parties, trying it first</td><td>Daily use, households that love the ice</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A simple rule: if you would go to Sonic for ice at least a couple of times a week, price a machine. If you want it once a month, buy a bag.</p>`],
      ['Route 3: DIY approximations', `<p>You cannot truly make nugget ice without a machine that flakes and compresses ice, as one nugget-ice explainer notes. You can, however, get a texture that is closer than whole cubes.</p><ul><li><b>Crushed ice from a blender or bag.</b> Pulse cubes in a blender or crush them in a bag. Pieces come out irregular, and they are harder and less airy than nuggets.</li><li><b>Crushing cubes from a refrigerator dispenser.</b> Many fridge dispensers offer a crushed setting. It is small and quick to use, but it is crushed cube ice, not compressed flakes.</li><li><b>Chopping nuggets.</b> You can roughly chop nugget ice in a food processor and topping it with flavored syrup for a shaved-ice-style dessert. That is a use for the real thing rather than a substitute.</li></ul><p>If your fridge has a nugget option, see our <a href="/best-refrigerator-with-nugget-ice/">refrigerators with nugget ice</a> page.</p>`],
      ['Getting the Sonic drink experience', `<p>Ice is half of it; the other half is the drink. Nugget ice suits sugary, flavored drinks such as dirty sodas, and it blends well into frozen cocktails. Because nuggets are small and melt quickly, avoid them in a cocktail or other drink with a subtle flavor.</p><ul><li><b>Fill the cup.</b> Pack ice to the top, then pour soda over it. A full cup keeps drinks colder and makes the ice last.</li><li><b>Use cold drinks.</b> Chilled soda, tea or lemonade melts less ice than room-temperature liquid.</li><li><b>Flavor the drink.</b> Add syrup, a splash of fruit puree or cream to the soda. Nugget ice absorbs flavor, so it pairs well with these additions.</li><li><b>Chew the leftovers.</b> This is the point of the ice.</li></ul>`],
      ['Water, cleaning and what to expect', `<p>A home machine makes ice from the water you give it, so the ice tastes like that water. If your tap water tastes strong, use filtered water. Nugget machines also build up mineral scale over time and need regular cleaning; check the manual for your model's cycle and how often to run it. Our guides on <a href="/how-to-clean-ge-opal-ice-maker/">cleaning an Opal</a> and <a href="/how-to-descale-ge-opal/">descaling an Opal</a> are a useful starting point even for other brands.</p><p>Expect a countertop machine to make a small batch at a time. Ice is made at the rate the output rating allows, and the bin holds only a few pounds. It will not match the volume at a drive-in, which is why a machine fits a household but not a crowd of fifty.</p>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['Is Sonic ice nugget ice?', 'Yes. Sonic serves pebble or nugget ice, made from compacted ice flakes. Sonic has used nugget ice since the mid-1980s, produced with a machine designed by Scotsman.'],
      ['Can I buy bags of Sonic ice?', 'Sonic sells 10-pound bags, and Chick-fil-A also sells bags of ice. Availability and pricing depend on the location, so call ahead.'],
      ['What is the best way to get Sonic ice at home every day?', 'A countertop nugget ice maker. It makes ice from tap or filtered water, and needs regular cleaning. See our best nugget ice maker page for options.'],
      ['Can I make Sonic ice without a nugget machine?', 'Not exactly. Nugget ice needs a machine that flakes and compresses ice. Crushing cubes gives a similar size but a harder, less airy texture.'],
      ['Why does nugget ice taste better in drinks?', 'The air pockets in each piece let flavor seep in, so the ice picks up the taste of the drink.'],
    ],
    refs: [['Food Republic, You can buy bags of the beloved nugget ice at this fast food chain', 'https://www.foodrepublic.com/1688423/sonic-popular-ice-nuggets/'], ['Nation\'s Restaurant News, Sonic Drive-In: the original beverage trendsetter', 'https://nrn.com/beverage-trends/sonic-drive-in-the-original-beverage-trend-setter'], ['Popular Mechanics, The best nugget ice makers', 'https://www.popularmechanics.com/home/a38304163/best-nugget-ice-makers/']],
    next: ['what-is-nugget-ice', 'best-nugget-ice-maker', 'best-cheap-nugget-ice-maker', 'best-refrigerator-with-nugget-ice', 'nugget-vs-bullet-ice-maker'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'commercial-ice-maker-rental-vs-buying', short: 'Ice maker rental vs buying', blurb: 'Renting, leasing or buying a commercial ice machine: costs, service, risk and a break-even formula.', 
    tab: 'Commercial Ice Maker: Rent, Lease or Buy? Costs and Break-Even',
    summary: 'Should you rent, lease or buy a commercial ice maker? Compare cash flow, service, repairs and lifespan, use a break-even formula, and see what to ask before signing.',
    heading: 'Commercial ice maker: rental vs buying',
    lede: 'A commercial ice machine can be rented, leased or bought outright. Each route moves money, repair risk and paperwork around in a different way. This page lays out the trade-offs, gives a break-even formula you can fill in yourself, and lists the questions to ask before you sign.',
    quick: 'Buy if you have the cash, a stable location and someone who will schedule cleaning and repairs. Rent or lease with service included if cash is tight, your location or volume may change, or you do not want to manage maintenance. Most of the cost of owning is spent after the purchase, so compare the total over several years, not only the sticker price.',
    list: ['Hoshizaki AM-50BAK', 'Manitowoc Indigo NXT undercounter', 'Scotsman Prodigy Plus undercounter', 'Whynter UIM-502SS', 'VEVOR Commercial Ice Maker (130 lb, 30 lb bin)'],
    parts: [
      ['The three routes in plain terms', `<table><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>How it works</th><th>Who keeps the repair risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Buy</td><td>Pay the purchase price, plus a bin and install if separate. You own the unit.</td><td>You, once any warranty ends</td></tr><tr><td>Rent or subscribe</td><td>Monthly fee, often with service bundled in. You return the unit at the end.</td><td>Usually the provider, within the contract terms</td></tr><tr><td>Lease or finance</td><td>A lender or dealer provides the equipment for monthly payments over a term. Some leases end with a purchase option, some with a return.</td><td>Depends on the lease; service may be bundled or not</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Lenders describe leasing as monthly payments spread over 24 to 60 months, with ownership at the end or a return of the equipment depending on the structure. Terms differ between providers, so the contract is the only source that counts.</p><p>A note on sources: much of the published material on this question comes from companies that rent, lease or sell ice machines. Their numbers are useful as inputs, but they are written to support one answer. Where we cite them below, we name the type of source.</p>`],
      ['What buying really costs', `<p>A cost guide from an ice machine rental company says the purchase price of popular commercial models ranges from $5,000 to $10,000, with the bin often sold separately. It also claims that the purchase price is only about 20 percent of the total cost of ownership, with the other 80 percent coming after installation. That ratio is the company's own claim, so treat it as a prompt to check your costs, not as a fact for your kitchen.</p><p>The categories of cost after purchase are real, though:</p><ul><li><b>Professional cleaning.</b> The same guide says manufacturers recommend at least two professional cleanings a year. See our <a href="/how-to-clean-commercial-ice-maker/">commercial ice maker cleaning guide</a> for what the work involves.</li><li><b>Repairs.</b> It cites an average repair cost of $708 in 2021, with some repairs reaching $2,000. One ice machine maker says small fixes may stay in the low hundreds of dollars, while compressors or control boards can cost more.</li><li><b>Warranty gaps.</b> The rental company says 80 percent of service calls during the warranty period are not covered. Read your warranty to see what it excludes, such as cleaning, filters and descaling.</li><li><b>Backup ice.</b> If the machine fails, you buy bagged ice until it is fixed.</li><li><b>Water and power.</b> Utilities run as long as the machine does.</li><li><b>Replacement.</b> The guide says the average commercial machine lasts seven to eight years with typical maintenance, and can last up to 20 with the best maintenance.</li></ul><p>The maker adds that a paid-off unit can keep running for many years, and that you decide when to replace it. Both views can hold: a well-kept machine pays off, and a neglected one does not.</p>`],
      ['What renting or leasing offers', `<ul><li><b>Low cash upfront.</b> The monthly payment replaces the lump sum. Lenders note that many full-service restaurants run on thin margins, so tying up cash in one machine can hurt.</li><li><b>Service may be included.</b> One maker says many providers include routine cleaning and basic service in the monthly payment, and that a provider usually sends a technician when something fails. The rental company makes a similar case for its subscription.</li><li><b>Easier to change.</b> At renewal you can move to a different size or ice type if your volume or menu changes.</li><li><b>A bigger total over time.</b> The maker says the total paid over several years often exceeds the cost of owning the same unit. You pay for predictability.</li></ul><p>The details matter more than the label. A rental that includes cleaning, repairs, filters and replacement during downtime is a different product from a lease that only covers the unit. Ask exactly what is covered, and get it in writing.</p>`],
      ['A break-even formula you can fill in', `<p>One maker offers a simple way to compare the two. Use your own quotes for each number:</p><ul><li>Monthly cost to own = purchase price / (12 x expected life in years) + yearly maintenance / 12 + yearly utilities / 12</li><li>Monthly cost to lease = lease payment + any extra maintenance / 12</li><li>Break-even years = purchase price / (12 x lease payment - yearly maintenance)</li></ul><p>If the break-even period is shorter than the time you expect to stay at the address, buying starts to look better. The example below uses made-up numbers to show how the arithmetic works. They are not prices or quotes.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Illustrative input</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Purchase price (including bin and install)</td><td>$6,000</td></tr><tr><td>Lease or rental payment, service included</td><td>$150 per month</td></tr><tr><td>Yearly maintenance if you own (cleaning, filters, a repair reserve)</td><td>$600</td></tr><tr><td>12 x lease payment</td><td>$1,800 per year</td></tr><tr><td>Break-even years = 6,000 / (1,800 - 600)</td><td>5 years</td></tr></tbody></table><p>With these inputs, buying wins only if you stay about five years or longer. Change any input and the answer moves, which is why you should run it with real quotes. If you expect the machine to last seven to eight years, as the rental company guide says is typical, buying would come out ahead in this example. If you expect to move or grow out of the machine in two or three years, renting would.</p><p>Also count what the formula leaves out: downtime, the time you spend finding a service company, and what a failure costs on your busiest night.</p>`],
      ['Tax and financing notes', `<p>One maker notes that many U.S. small businesses can deduct qualifying equipment in the first year under Section 179 of the Internal Revenue Code, with limits that change by tax year. The rental company says rental payments are tax-deductible. Both claims depend on your business and the rules in force when you buy or sign, so check with a tax professional before you decide.</p><p>If you finance, lenders and leasing companies generally look at time in business, recent revenue and credit, according to the maker. Keep simple financial statements ready, and be ready to explain how the machine supports sales.</p>`],
      ['Match the machine to your volume first', `<p>Pick the machine before you pick the payment method. One maker gives these rough ranges for daily ice use: small cafes and bakeries often use 50 to 150 lb a day, and busy bars and casual restaurants 200 to 500 lb. A lender lists self-contained machines at about 50 to 400 lb per day and modular units at 300 to 1,000-plus lb. An oversized machine wastes money, and an undersized one runs out on your busiest night.</p><p>Renting makes sizing mistakes easier to fix, since you can swap the unit. If you buy, get the volume right the first time. Our pages on <a href="/best-small-commercial-ice-maker/">small commercial ice makers</a>, <a href="/best-undercounter-commercial-ice-maker/">undercounter commercial ice makers</a> and <a href="/best-ice-maker-for-coffee-shop/">ice makers for coffee shops</a> cover common sizes.</p>${pick('hoshizakiam50bak', 'A compact undercounter to price', 'Hoshizaki lists 54 lb per day and a 27 lb bin. A small cafe or bar with light ice demand could size it against its own use. See the product page for specifications.')}${pick('whynter502', 'Built-in or freestanding option', 'Rated at 80 lb per day with a 25 lb bin, suited to a home bar or very light commercial use.')}<p>For larger lists, see <a href="/best-commercial-ice-maker/">best commercial ice maker</a>. The ice type matters too. One equipment dealer guide says to choose the ice format your menu needs before comparing equipment. If you serve nugget ice, see our <a href="/best-commercial-nugget-ice-maker/">commercial nugget ice maker</a> page.</p>`],
      ['A one-page cost worksheet', `<p>Before you talk to a dealer or a rental company, fill in this list for each option. Using the same lines for every quote makes the comparison fair, and it shows which costs a quote leaves out.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Buy</th><th>Rent or lease</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Machine and bin</td><td>Purchase price</td><td>Monthly payment times the term</td></tr><tr><td>Delivery and install</td><td>Quote from dealer or installer</td><td>Ask whether it is included</td></tr><tr><td>Water filter and cartridges</td><td>Your cost each year</td><td>Ask whether it is included</td></tr><tr><td>Professional cleaning</td><td>At least two visits a year, per the manufacturer guidance cited above</td><td>Ask whether it is included</td></tr><tr><td>Repairs</td><td>A reserve you set aside</td><td>Ask what the contract covers</td></tr><tr><td>Backup ice during downtime</td><td>Your cost</td><td>Ask about a loaner or credit</td></tr><tr><td>End of term</td><td>Resale or replacement</td><td>Return, renew or buy out</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Add the columns over the number of years you plan to stay. The lower total is the cheaper route on paper. Then weigh the soft factors: how much of your own time each route takes, and how you would cope with a failure on a Friday night.</p>`],
      ['Who should do what', `<table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Leaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stable location, spare cash, staff who keep up with cleaning</td><td>Buy</td></tr><tr><td>Thin cash, new business, unsure of volume</td><td>Rent or lease with service</td></tr><tr><td>Short lease on your space, or plans to move</td><td>Rent or lease</td></tr><tr><td>Small home-style use, such as a home bar</td><td>Buy a lower-cost unit; renting rarely fits</td></tr><tr><td>Past breakdowns left you scrambling</td><td>A service-included contract</td></tr><tr><td>You already pay for a service plan on other equipment</td><td>Buy and add this machine to the plan</td></tr></tbody></table>`],
      ['Questions to ask before you sign', `<ul><li>What is the total over the full term, including fees, taxes and delivery?</li><li>Are cleaning, filters, repairs, parts and labor included? Are there limits or exclusions?</li><li>How fast does a technician arrive, and what happens to your ice supply while the unit is down?</li><li>Who installs it, and who pays for plumbing, drain and electrical work?</li><li>Can you upgrade, swap or return the unit mid-term, and at what cost?</li><li>Is there a buyout option at the end, and what is the price?</li><li>What is the notice period and penalty for ending early?</li><li>Does the unit meet the sanitation and certification rules for your kind of business? Ask your health department what it requires.</li></ul><p>If you buy, ask the same service questions of your dealer, and ask the manufacturer what the warranty covers. Warranties often exclude routine items, so do not assume repairs are free.</p>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['Is it cheaper to rent or buy a commercial ice machine?', 'It depends on how long you keep it and what service costs. Buying usually costs less over a long period, if you maintain it. Renting costs more in total but spreads out cash and may include service. Use the break-even formula with real quotes.'],
      ['How long does a commercial ice machine last?', 'A rental company says the average lasts seven to eight years and can reach 20 with the best maintenance. Your water quality and upkeep affect this.'],
      ['Does renting include maintenance?', 'Often, but not always. One maker says many providers include routine cleaning and basic service. Confirm what is covered, including filters and repairs.'],
      ['What size ice machine does a restaurant need?', 'One maker gives rough ranges of 50 to 150 lb per day for small cafes and bakeries, and 200 to 500 lb for busy bars and casual restaurants. Measure your own use first.'],
      ['Should a home bar rent an ice machine?', 'Rarely. Rental programs are built for businesses. A home bar is usually better served by buying a smaller unit; see our home bar ice maker page.'],
    ],
    refs: [['Modern Restaurant Management, The true cost of ice machine ownership (Easy Ice)', 'https://modernrestaurantmanagement.com/the-true-cost-of-ice-machine-ownership/'], ['Euhomy, Leasing vs buying a commercial ice maker', 'https://www.euhomy.com/blogs/inspiration/leasing-vs-buying-commercial-ice-maker'], ['Crestmont Capital, Leasing ice machines and beverage dispensers for restaurants', 'https://www.crestmontcapital.com/blog/leasing-ice-machines-and-beverage-dispensers-for-restaurants'], ['The Restaurant Warehouse, Commercial ice machine guide', 'https://therestaurantwarehouse.com/blogs/restaurant-equipment/ice-machine-leasing'], ['ChefsDeal, Is an ice machine lease the right choice', 'https://www.chefsdeal.com/blog/ice-machine-lease-right-choice']],
    next: ['best-commercial-ice-maker', 'best-small-commercial-ice-maker', 'best-undercounter-commercial-ice-maker', 'how-to-clean-commercial-ice-maker', 'best-ice-maker-for-coffee-shop'],
  },
  {
    plain: true, path: 'ge-opal-water-filter', short: 'GE Opal water filter', blurb: 'GE Opal water filter guide: carbon vs scale-inhibiting, how to replace it, how often, and the rear filter.', 
    tab: 'GE Opal Water Filter Guide: Replace, Types and Rear Filter Care',
    summary: 'GE Opal water filter guide: carbon vs scale-inhibiting filters, how to install one, how often to replace it, and how the separate rear filter differs.',
    heading: 'GE Opal water filter guide',
    lede: 'Your GE Profile Opal has more than one filter, and people often mix them up. One sits in the water reservoir and cleans the water that becomes ice. Another sits at the back and keeps dust out of the machine. Here is what each one does, how to change it and how to keep the ice tasting fresh.',
    quick: 'The reservoir filter for the Opal is GE part P4INKFILTR (carbon), and GE recommends replacing it every three months. A scale-inhibiting version, P4INKSFLTR, is aimed at hard water. The rear filter is a separate, washable part that GE says to check and clean at least every two months. Never run the Opal without it.',
    list: ['GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra', 'GE Profile Opal 2.0', 'GE Profile Opal 2.0 XL'],
    parts: [
      ['Two different filters on an Opal', `<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Water filter (reservoir)</th><th>Rear filter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where</td><td>Inside the lower water reservoir</td><td>On the back panel of the machine</td></tr><tr><td>What it does</td><td>Filters the water used to make ice</td><td>Keeps dirt, dust and lint out of the condenser</td></tr><tr><td>Care</td><td>Replace it</td><td>Wash it and put it back</td></tr><tr><td>GE interval</td><td>Every three months</td><td>Check and clean at least every two months</td></tr></tbody></table><p>GE's support pages treat these as separate parts. The reservoir filter is a consumable you buy. The rear filter is washed in warm, soapy water and reused.</p>`],
      ['Carbon and scale-inhibiting: which one', `<p>GE lists two water filter accessories for the Opal. Part P4INKFILTR is the Opal carbon filter. Retailer listings say it removes the taste and odor of chlorine and is made of plastic and carbon. GE's support page says the filter helps ice taste fresher, and that using it with distilled water also helps prevent hard-mineral scale and taste changes.</p><p>Part P4INKSFLTR is GE's Opal scale-inhibiting filter. GE's listing does not describe how it works, so we cannot tell you more than its name suggests: it is meant to deal with scale. Our <a href="/ge-opal-vs-opal-2/">Opal vs Opal 2.0 comparison</a> notes that the 2.0 Ultra comes with the scale-inhibiting filter, and the 2.0 and 2.0 XL come with a carbon filter.</p><table><thead><tr><th>If your situation is</th><th>Start with</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Water tastes of chlorine</td><td>Carbon filter</td></tr><tr><td>Hard water, white scale in the reservoir</td><td>Scale-inhibiting filter, plus regular descaling</td></tr><tr><td>You use distilled water</td><td>Still fit the filter, per GE's own guidance</td></tr><tr><td>Your model is the Opal Mini</td><td>Check the Mini's own filter part number</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Opal Mini uses a different part. GE's parts site lists a separate Mini scale-inhibiting filter, P4NMKSAZAA. Confirm the number against your manual before you order.</p>${pick('opalultra', 'Opal with the scale-inhibiting filter included', 'It arrives with the scale-inhibiting filter, which suits hard-water homes. Rated up to 38 lb a day on current GE listings, with a 3 lb bin.')}`],
      ['How often to replace the reservoir filter', `<p>GE says: "We recommend replacing the water filter accessory every three months for maximum freshness." Retail listings for the carbon filter repeat the three-month figure. GE's listing for the scale-inhibiting filter does not give an interval, so use your owner's manual for that one.</p><p>Three months means four filters a year. If you want to be ready, here is a simple schedule.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Months since install</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>Install the filter and write the date on the back of the machine or in your phone</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Check and wash the rear filter</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Replace the reservoir filter</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Wash the rear filter again</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Replace the filter again and plan a descale; see the descaling guide</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If your Opal is on the SmartHQ app, the 2.0 can show maintenance alerts and reorder accessories, per GE's listing for the 2.0. Whether or not you use the app, a calendar reminder works well.</p>`],
      ['How to replace the reservoir filter', `<p>These steps come from GE's support page for the Opal water filter accessory:</p><ol><li>Remove the included screen from the ice machine.</li><li>Hold the new filter with the fins facing up.</li><li>Pinch the fins with two fingers and slide the nozzle end into the hole at the back left of the lower reservoir.</li><li>Slide the filter base against the tab near the middle of the back side, and push down to seat it.</li></ol><p>To remove an old filter, twist it toward you and lift it out. Retail listings say replacing it needs no tools. GE notes that if you connect a side tank, its water passes through the bottom reservoir where the filter sits, so water from both sources is filtered.</p><p>Empty the old water, rinse the reservoir, and run the machine as normal. If ice quality seems off after a swap, see <a href="/ge-opal-not-making-ice/">Opal not making ice</a>.</p>`],
      ['Cleaning the rear filter', `<p>The rear filter is easy to forget because it is at the back. GE's support page for the Opal 2.0 Ultra gives these steps:</p><ol><li>The filter is attached to the back panel. Pull it up and out gently by the handle.</li><li>Wash it in warm, soapy water, then rinse.</li><li>Let it dry completely before you put it back.</li></ol><p>GE says to check and clean it at least every two months. It warns not to put the filter in the dishwasher, and not to run the Opal without it, because dirt, dust and lint can clog the condenser and reduce performance. If your kitchen is dusty or the machine sits near a dryer vent or pet bedding, check it more often.</p>`],
      ['Does the filter replace descaling?', `<p>No. The filter works on the water going in. Mineral scale still builds up inside a nugget machine over time, particularly in hard water. GE offers an Opal cleaning kit for descaling. Follow your manual for the schedule. Our guides to <a href="/how-to-descale-ge-opal/">descaling a GE Opal</a> and <a href="/how-to-clean-ge-opal-ice-maker/">cleaning a GE Opal</a> walk through the steps.</p><p>Think of the filter as lowering the load and the descale as clearing what gets through. A fresh filter does not mean you can skip a descale, and a descale does not mean you can skip the filter.</p>`],
      ['Buying a replacement', `<ul><li><b>Check availability.</b> GE's own pages for both P4INKFILTR and P4INKSFLTR currently say the model is no longer being manufactured, and some retailers list them as out of stock. Stock changes, so look at several sellers.</li><li><b>Match the number.</b> Buy by part number, not by the word "Opal" alone, because the Mini uses its own part.</li><li><b>Third-party filters.</b> Third-party sellers list filters described as compatible with GE Opal machines, including multi-packs. We have not verified fit, flow or filtering claims for these. Check the seller's compatibility list and the manual, and think about whether it affects your warranty.</li><li><b>Buy for the year.</b> Four filters cover a year at the GE interval, so a multi-pack can make sense if the price is fair.</li></ul><p>For other nugget makers and what they use, see our <a href="/best-ice-maker-water-filter/">ice maker water filter</a> roundup and <a href="/best-water-for-ice-maker/">best water for ice makers</a> page.</p>`],
      ['Troubleshooting filter problems', `<table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>What to try</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ice tastes stale or of chlorine</td><td>Filter is past its interval</td><td>Replace the reservoir filter; empty and rinse the reservoir</td></tr><tr><td>White flakes or crust in the reservoir</td><td>Mineral scale from hard water</td><td>Descale; consider the scale-inhibiting filter</td></tr><tr><td>Machine runs warm or ice output drops</td><td>Rear filter clogged with dust</td><td>Wash the rear filter and let it dry fully</td></tr><tr><td>Filter will not seat</td><td>Screen still in place, or fins pointing the wrong way</td><td>Remove the screen and follow the install steps</td></tr><tr><td>Machine will not make ice at all</td><td>Not usually the filter</td><td>See the Opal not making ice guide</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The first two rows follow from GE's guidance on freshness and distilled water. The last three are our own suggestions based on GE's instructions, not GE's published troubleshooting table.</p>`],
    ],
    asked: [
      ['What filter does the GE Opal use?', 'GE lists P4INKFILTR as the Opal water filter accessory, a carbon filter, and P4INKSFLTR as the scale-inhibiting filter. The Opal Mini has its own part number. Check your manual.'],
      ['How often should I change the Opal water filter?', 'GE recommends replacing the water filter accessory every three months for maximum freshness.'],
      ['Can I run the Opal without a filter?', 'GE warns not to run the Opal without the rear filter, since dust and lint can clog the condenser. For the water filter, GE recommends using it for fresher ice.'],
      ['How do I clean the Opal rear filter?', 'Pull it up and out by the handle, wash it in warm soapy water, rinse it, and let it dry fully before replacing it. Do not use the dishwasher. GE says to clean it at least every two months.'],
      ['Does the Opal 2.0 Ultra include a filter?', 'It comes with a scale-inhibiting filter, per GE\'s model listing. The 2.0 and 2.0 XL come with a carbon filter.'],
    ],
    refs: [['GE Appliances, Opal Nugget Ice Maker filter, parts and accessories support page', 'https://products.geappliances.com/appliance/gea-support-search-content?contentId=35280'], ['GE Appliances, Opal 2.0 Ultra rear filter cleaning', 'https://products.geappliances.com/appliance/gea-support-search-content?contentId=000064597'], ['GE Appliances, Opal Carbon Filter P4INKFILTR', 'https://www.geappliances.com/appliance/GE-Profile-Opal-Nugget-Ice-Maker-Water-Filter-Accessory-P4INKFILTR'], ['GE Appliances, Opal Scale Inhibiting Filter P4INKSFLTR', 'https://www.geappliances.com/appliance/GE-Profile-Scale-Inhibiting-Filter-P4INKSFLTR'], ['GE Appliance Parts, Opal Mini scale inhibiting filter', 'https://www.geapplianceparts.com/store/parts/spec/P4NMKSAZAA']],
    next: ['ge-opal-vs-opal-2', 'how-to-descale-ge-opal', 'how-to-clean-ge-opal-ice-maker', 'ge-opal-not-making-ice', 'ge-profile-opal-2-review'],
  },
];
